1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,612
Chapter 14: Obstetrical Imaging.

3
00:00:05,612 --> 00:00:22,287
Technology And Safety Gestational Age Assessment First-Trimester Ultrasound Second- And Third-Trimester Ultrasound Placenta And Cervix Hydramnios Oligohydramnios Doppler Magnetic Resonance Imaging References Obstetrical ultrasound is fundamental to prenatal care.

4
00:00:22,287 --> 00:00:35,112
It is used to confirm gestational age and viability; to detect and characterize abnormalities of the fetus, amnionic fluid, and placenta; and to assist with diagnostic and therapeutic procedures.

5
00:00:35,112 --> 00:00:38,387
Ultrasound practice continues to evolve.

6
00:00:38,387 --> 00:00:45,775
The number of components included in the second-trimester standard and detailed fetal anatomical surveys has expanded.

7
00:00:45,775 --> 00:00:53,087
With improved image resolution, fetal abnormalities are increasingly identified in the late first trimester.

8
00:00:53,087 --> 00:01:04,475
This has prompted the requirement for a limited anatomical survey during the standard first- trimester examination and has led to the development of a new detailed firsttrimester examination.

9
00:01:04,475 --> 00:01:13,100
Detailed placental evaluation is a new specialized examination to aid detection and characterization of placenta accreta spectrum.

10
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:26,162
Across the United States, pregnant women receive ultrasound examinations in various practice settings staffed by obstetrician– gynecologists, maternal–fetal medicine specialists, and radiologists.

11
00:01:26,162 --> 00:01:43,050
Ideally, examinations are performed by registered diagnostic medical sonographers or physicians with certification in their area(s) of practice and in units accredited by the American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine (AIUM) or American College of Radiology.

12
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:56,075
Components of accreditation include evidence of physician training, sonographer credentialing, continuing medical education, and protocols and procedures to ensure proper and safe ultrasound practice.

13
00:01:56,075 --> 00:02:00,725
One important component is independent review of submitted images.

14
00:02:00,725 --> 00:02:22,062
To standardize ultrasound education for residents in obstetrics and gynecology and fellows in maternal-fetal medicine, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, and the AIUM have developed consensus documents (Abuhamad, 2018; Benacerraf, 2018).

15
00:02:22,062 --> 00:02:32,325
A focus of training—and of ultrasound practice—is standardization of an ultrasound curriculum and provision of competency assessment tools for image acquisition.

16
00:02:32,325 --> 00:02:34,650
Technology And Safety.

17
00:02:34,650 --> 00:02:44,675
The image on the ultrasound screen is produced by sound waves that are reflected back from fluid and tissue interfaces of the fetus, amnionic fluid, and placenta.

18
00:02:44,675 --> 00:02:55,350
Ultrasound transducers contain groups of piezoelectric crystals that convert electrical energy into sound waves and convert returning sound waves back into electrical energy.

19
00:02:55,350 --> 00:02:59,012
The sound waves are emitted in synchronized pulses.

20
00:02:59,012 --> 00:03:06,050
As these pulses pass through tissue layers, dense tissue such as bone produces high-velocity reflected waves.

21
00:03:06,050 --> 00:03:14,900
With routine grayscale imaging, which is also known as brightness-mode (B-mode), these reflected waves are displayed as bright echoes on the screen.

22
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:19,862
Conversely, fluid generates few reflected waves and appears dark.

23
00:03:19,862 --> 00:03:28,800
Digital images generated at 50 to more than 100 frames per second undergo postprocessing that yields the appearance of real-time imaging.

24
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:36,287
Ultrasound refers to sound waves traveling at a frequency above 20,000 hertz (Hz [cycles per second]).

25
00:03:36,287 --> 00:03:42,000
Transducers use wide-bandwidth technology to perform within a range of frequencies.

26
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:49,875
Higher-frequency transducers yield better image resolution, whereas lower frequencies penetrate tissue more effectively.

27
00:03:49,875 --> 00:04:02,062
In early pregnancy, a 5- to 12-megahertz (MHz) transvaginal transducer usually provides excellent resolution, because the early fetus lies close to the transducer.

28
00:04:02,062 --> 00:04:12,162
In the first and second trimesters, a 4- to 6-MHz transabdominal transducer is similarly sufficiently close to the fetus to yield precise images.

29
00:04:12,162 --> 00:04:25,587
By the third trimester, however, a lower-frequency 2- to 5MHz transducer may be needed for tissue penetration—particularly in obese patients—and this can lead to compromised image resolution.

30
00:04:25,587 --> 00:04:28,637
# Embryo and Fetal Safety.

31
00:04:28,637 --> 00:04:33,812
Sonography should be performed only for a valid medical indication and use

32
00:04:33,964 --> 00:04:38,751
the lowest possible exposure setting to gain necessary information.

33
00:04:38,701 --> 00:04:43,064
This is the ALARA principle—as low as reasonably achievable.

34
00:04:43,064 --> 00:05:04,251
Examinations are performed only by those trained to recognize fetal abnormalities and artifacts that may mimic pathology and with techniques to avoid ultrasound exposure beyond what is considered safe for the fetus (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020; American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine, 2018a).

35
00:05:04,251 --> 00:05:12,601
No causal relationship has been demonstrated between diagnostic ultrasound and any recognized adverse effect in human pregnancy.

36
00:05:12,601 --> 00:05:29,064
The International Society of Ultrasound in Obstetrics and Gynecology (2016) further concludes that there is no scientifically proven association between ultrasound exposure in the first or second trimesters and autism spectrum disorder or its severity.

37
00:05:29,064 --> 00:05:36,201
All ultrasound machines are required to display two indices: the thermal index and the mechanical index.

38
00:05:36,201 --> 00:05:43,614
The thermal index measures the relative probability that the examination may raise the temperature enough to induce injury.

39
00:05:43,614 --> 00:05:51,726
However, fetal damage resulting from commercially available ultrasound equipment in routine practice is extremely unlikely.

40
00:05:51,726 --> 00:05:59,214
The potential for temperature elevation is higher with longer examination time and is greater near bone than in soft tissue.

41
00:05:59,214 --> 00:06:04,239
Theoretical risks are higher during organogenesis than later in gestation.

42
00:06:04,239 --> 00:06:13,364
The thermal index for soft tissue, Tis, should be used before 10 weeks’ gestation, and that for bone, Tib, at or beyond 10 weeks .

43
00:06:13,364 --> 00:06:19,301
The thermal index is higher with pulsed Doppler applications than with routine B-mode scanning.

44
00:06:19,301 --> 00:06:30,601
In the first trimester, if pulsed Doppler is clinically indicated, the thermal index should be ≤0.7, and the exposure time should be as brief as possible .

45
00:06:30,601 --> 00:06:41,576
This is an important consideration when pulsed Doppler is applied to assist with identification or characterization of suspected abnormalities at 11 to 14 weeks’ gestation.

46
00:06:41,576 --> 00:06:49,651
To document the embryonic or fetal heart rate, motionmode (M-mode) scanning is used instead of pulsed Doppler imaging.

47
00:06:49,651 --> 00:07:00,389
The mechanical index is a measure of the likelihood of adverse effects related to rarefactional pressure, such as cavitation, which is relevant only in tissues that contain air.

48
00:07:00,389 --> 00:07:06,001
Microbubble ultrasound contrast agents are not used in pregnancy for this reason.

49
00:07:06,001 --> 00:07:14,714
In mammalian tissues that do not contain gas bodies, no adverse effects have been reported over the range of diagnostically relevant exposures.

50
00:07:14,714 --> 00:07:19,576
Fetuses cannot contain gas bodies and thus are not considered at risk.

51
00:07:19,576 --> 00:07:42,889
The use of ultrasound for any nonmedical purpose, such as “keepsake fetal imaging,” is considered contrary to responsible medical practice and is not condoned by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) (2019), the American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine (2020b), or the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b).

52
00:07:42,889 --> 00:07:50,289
However, images or video clips from medically indicated ultrasound examinations may be shared with patients.

53
00:07:50,289 --> 00:07:52,876
# Operator Safety.

54
00:07:52,876 --> 00:08:04,714
The reported prevalence of work-related musculoskeletal discomfort or injury among sonographers approximates 70 percent (Janga, 2012; Roll, 2012).

55
00:08:04,714 --> 00:08:15,076
The most common injuries are capsulitis and tendonitis of the shoulder, epicondylitis of the elbow, carpal and cubital tunnel syndrome, and neck or back strain .

56
00:08:15,076 --> 00:08:26,676
The main risk factors for injury during transabdominal ultrasound examinations are awkward posture, sustained static forces, and various pinch grips used to maneuver the transducer .

57
00:08:26,676 --> 00:08:32,926
Excessive flexion, extension, or abduction while scanning places stress on joints and muscles.

58
00:08:32,926 --> 00:08:37,889
Task repetition without adequate recovery time may compound risks.

59
00:08:37,889 --> 00:08:44,339
Maternal habitus can be contributory because more force is often needed when imaging obese patients.

60
00:08:44,339 --> 00:08:51,564
The following guidelines may help avert injury: 1. Position the patient close to you on the examination table.

61
00:08:51,564 --> 00:08:59,339
As a result, your elbow is close to your body, shoulder abduction is <30 degrees, and your thumb is facing up.

62
00:08:59,339 --> 00:09:04,751
2. Adjust the table or chair height so that your forearm is parallel to the floor.

63
00:09:04,751 --> 00:09:06,901
3. Use a

64
00:09:07,060 --> 00:09:09,960
chair with back support, if seated.

65
00:09:09,910 --> 00:09:13,072
Avoid leaning toward the patient or monitor.

66
00:09:13,072 --> 00:09:17,047
Support your feet, and keep ankles in neutral position.

67
00:09:17,047 --> 00:09:25,560
4. Face the monitor squarely and position it so that it is viewed at a neutral angle from the horizon, such as 15 degrees downward.

68
00:09:25,560 --> 00:09:30,097
5. Avoid reaching, bending, or twisting while scanning.

69
00:09:30,097 --> 00:09:34,347
6. Take frequent breaks to help prevent muscle strain.

70
00:09:34,347 --> 00:09:38,135
Stretching and strengthening exercises can be helpful.

71
00:09:38,135 --> 00:09:40,660
Gestational Age Assessment.

72
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:52,135
Gestational age is based on two things: the certainty of the woman’s last menstrual period (LMP) date and measurements of the embryo or fetus at the initial ultrasound examination.

73
00:09:52,135 --> 00:09:57,210
Gestational sac measurement is not suitable for gestational age assignment.

74
00:09:57,210 --> 00:10:13,447
If the LMP is certain, the estimated due date (EDD) is based on LMP unless the date–measurement discrepancy exceeds thresholds listed in Table 14-1 (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019b,c).

75
00:10:13,447 --> 00:10:22,322
If the discrepancy exceeds these thresholds, or if the LMP is uncertain or unknown, ultrasound measurements establish the EDD.

76
00:10:22,322 --> 00:10:24,772
Table 14-1.

77
00:10:24,772 --> 00:10:37,247
Assessment of Gestational Age Ultrasound measurement of the crown-rump length (CRL) is the most accurate method to establish or confirm gestational age (Appendix, p. 1234).

78
00:10:37,247 --> 00:10:41,435
Transvaginal imaging yields higher resolution images.

79
00:10:41,435 --> 00:10:48,397
The CRL is measured in the midsagittal plane with the embryo or fetus in a neutral, nonflexed position.

80
00:10:48,397 --> 00:10:52,122
This allows its length to be measured in a straight line .

81
00:10:52,122 --> 00:10:55,435
The mean of three discrete measurements is used.

82
00:10:55,435 --> 00:11:01,672
Before 14 weeks’ gestation, the CRL is accurate to within 5 to 7 days .

83
00:11:01,672 --> 00:11:04,147
Figure 14-1.

84
00:11:04,147 --> 00:11:10,322
A. The measured crown-rump length is approximately 7 mm in this 6-week embryo.

85
00:11:10,322 --> 00:11:17,572
B. M-mode demonstrates embryonic cardiac activity and a heart rate of 124 beats per minute.

86
00:11:17,572 --> 00:11:27,510
Starting at 140/7 weeks’ gestation, the biparietal diameter, head circumference, abdominal circumference, and femur length should be measured.

87
00:11:27,510 --> 00:11:35,310
Equipment software formulas calculate estimated gestational age and fetal weight from these four biometric parameters.

88
00:11:35,310 --> 00:11:41,497
The accuracy of the fetal weight estimate is assumed to be within 15 percent of the actual weight .

89
00:11:41,497 --> 00:11:47,222
Measurement criteria are discussed in Chapter 15 (p. 272).

90
00:11:47,222 --> 00:11:55,822
Before 22 weeks, gestational age assessment using these four biometric parameters is accurate to within 7 to 10 days .

91
00:11:55,822 --> 00:12:09,397
Various nomograms are available for other structural measurements, including the transverse cerebellar diameter, orbital distances, thoracic circumference, and length of the ear, kidney, long bones, and feet.

92
00:12:09,397 --> 00:12:19,460
These may be used to address specific questions regarding organ system abnormalities or syndromes (Appendix, pp. 1238– 1241).

93
00:12:19,460 --> 00:12:27,885
If the initial ultrasound examination is performed at or beyond 22 weeks’ gestation, the pregnancy is suboptimally dated .

94
00:12:27,885 --> 00:12:34,335
In such cases, subsequent ultrasound evaluation in 3 to 4 weeks may be considered.

95
00:12:34,335 --> 00:12:43,922
This is especially true if the ultrasound measurements are smaller than expected for gestational age based on LMP and thus poor fetal growth is a possibility.

96
00:12:43,922 --> 00:12:49,097
Fertilization is presumed to occur 2 weeks after a confident LMP.

97
00:12:49,097 --> 00:13:00,960
Therefore, for pregnancies achieved with in vitro fertilization and fresh transfer, 266 days are added to the egg-retrieval/fertilization date to calculate the EDD.

98
00:13:00,960 --> 00:13:08,910
Similarly, if using a day-3 frozen embryo, adding 263 days accounts for the days of embryo culture.

99
00:13:08,910 --> 00:13:14,422
For pregnancies conceived with intrauterine insemination, LMP is used.

100
00:13:14,422 --> 00:13:17,135
First-Trimester Ultrasound.

101
00:13:17,135 --> 00:13:33,135
The three types of first-trimester examinations include standard ultrasound; nuchal translucency evaluation—between 11 and 14 weeks’ gestation; and detailed first-trimester anatomy evaluation between 12 and 14 weeks’ gestation.

102
00:13:33,135 --> 00:13:47,835
Indications for the standard first-trimester examination are listed in Table 14-2 (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020; American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine, 2018a).

103
00:13:47,835 --> 00:13:54,285
Early pregnancy can be evaluated with transabdominal or transvaginal sonography, or both.

104
00:13:54,285 --> 00:14:03,922
The components listed in Table 14-3 should be assessed (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020; American

105
00:14:04,084 --> 00:14:08,246
Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine, 2018a).

106
00:14:08,196 --> 00:14:14,509
The first trimester is the ideal time to evaluate the uterus, adnexa, and cul-de-sac.

107
00:14:14,509 --> 00:14:25,359
Ultrasound interrogation at this time can reliably diagnose anembryonic gestation, embryonic demise, ectopic pregnancy, and gestational trophoblastic disease.

108
00:14:25,359 --> 00:14:32,096
In a multifetal gestation, determination of chorionicity is most accurate in the first trimester .

109
00:14:32,096 --> 00:14:39,584
Gestational sac implantation in a prior cesarean scar is increasingly detected in the first trimester as well.

110
00:14:39,584 --> 00:14:42,059
Table 14-2.

111
00:14:42,059 --> 00:14:47,896
Indications for First-Trimester Ultrasound Examination Table 14-3.

112
00:14:47,896 --> 00:15:02,534
Components of Standard Ultrasound Examination by Trimester An intrauterine gestational sac may be consistently visualized with transvaginal sonography by 5 weeks’ gestation, and an embryo with cardiac activity by 6 weeks.

113
00:15:02,534 --> 00:15:12,009
The embryo should be visible transvaginally once the mean sac diameter has reached 25 mm—otherwise the gestation is anembryonic.

114
00:15:12,009 --> 00:15:23,484
Whenever an embryo or fetus is identified, it should be measured, and cardiac motion should be documented with either video clip or M-mode scanning (see Fig. 14-1B).

115
00:15:23,484 --> 00:15:33,196
Cardiac motion may be visible with transvaginal imaging when the length of the embryo is 2 mm and should be visible at 7 mm in a live embryo .

116
00:15:33,196 --> 00:15:42,321
If an embryo measures <7 mm and has no visible cardiac activity, subsequent examination is recommended in 1 week .

117
00:15:42,321 --> 00:15:54,984
At Parkland Hospital, first-trimester demise is diagnosed with transvaginal ultrasound if the embryo has reached 10 mm and lacks cardiac motion, taking measurement error into consideration.

118
00:15:54,984 --> 00:16:02,021
Criteria to diagnose first-trimester demise are found in Table 11-2 (p. 202).

119
00:16:02,021 --> 00:16:10,771
A recent addition to the standard first-trimester examination is an assessment of selected anatomical components when fetal size permits.

120
00:16:10,771 --> 00:16:17,871
These include the calvarium, umbilical cord insertion into the ventral wall, and presence of extremities .

121
00:16:17,871 --> 00:16:20,771
# Nuchal Translucency.

122
00:16:20,771 --> 00:16:30,271
This represents the maximum thickness of the subcutaneous translucent area between the skin and soft tissue overlying the fetal spine at the back of the neck.

123
00:16:30,271 --> 00:16:48,646
The nuchal translucency (NT) is measured in the sagittal plane between 11 and 14 weeks’ gestation using precise criteria (American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine, 2018a; International Society of Ultrasound in Obstetrics and Gynecology, 2016).

124
00:16:48,646 --> 00:16:58,771
When the NT measurement is increased, the risk for fetal aneuploidy and various structural anomalies— in particular heart defects—is significantly elevated.

125
00:16:58,771 --> 00:17:06,734
It is a component of first-trimester aneuploidy screening, which is discussed in Chapter 17 (p. 337).

126
00:17:06,734 --> 00:17:11,334
The aneuploidy risk calculation depends on the crown-rump length.

127
00:17:11,334 --> 00:17:24,521
However, an NT measurement ≥3 mm is associated with increased risk for fetal structural or genetic abnormalities and is an indication for a detailed fetal anatomical survey.

128
00:17:24,521 --> 00:17:27,009
Table 14-4.

129
00:17:27,009 --> 00:17:34,734
Guidelines for Nuchal Translucency (NT) Measurement # Detailed First-Trimester Ultrasound Examination.

130
00:17:34,734 --> 00:17:44,234
Assessment for fetal abnormalities in an at-risk pregnancy may include a detailed survey of fetal anatomy at 12 to 14 weeks’ gestation.

131
00:17:44,234 --> 00:17:53,621
Such examinations are a recent addition and limited to specialized centers with advanced imaging skills, although use may be expected to expand.

132
00:17:53,621 --> 00:18:04,721
Despite improvements in imaging technology, it is not realistic to expect that all major abnormalities detectable in the second trimester may be visualized in the first trimester.

133
00:18:04,721 --> 00:18:11,159
Abnormalities may change in appearance as the fetus develops, and new findings may become evident.

134
00:18:11,159 --> 00:18:22,971
If an abnormality is identified in the first trimester, detailed second-trimester sonography will be important to further characterize the findings or identify associated abnormalities.

135
00:18:22,971 --> 00:18:33,234
In an at-risk pregnancy, a normal first-trimester examination does not obviate the recommendation for a detailed second-trimester fetal anatomical survey.

136
00:18:33,234 --> 00:18:45,996
In a systematic review of more than 118,000 pregnancies undergoing fetal NT assessment, 46 percent of major abnormalities were detected in low-risk or unselected pregnancies .

137
00:18:45,996 --> 00:18:51,046
In high-risk pregnancies, anomaly detection exceeded 60 percent.

138
00:18:51,046 --> 00:18:53,334
Detection rates are high

139
00:18:53,500 --> 00:18:59,375
for fetal anencephaly, alobar holoprosencephaly, and ventral wall defects.

140
00:18:59,325 --> 00:19:10,825
However, in one series of 40,000 pregnancies with fetal anatomical evaluation at the time of NT assessment, only a third of major cardiac anomalies were identified.

141
00:19:10,825 --> 00:19:22,387
No cases of microcephaly, agenesis of the corpus callosum, cerebellar abnormalities, congenital pulmonary airway malformations, or bowel obstruction were detected .

142
00:19:22,387 --> 00:19:30,437
Some of these abnormalities had not yet developed by the end of the first trimester, and this is an important caveat for counseling.

143
00:19:30,437 --> 00:19:33,637
Second- And Third-Trimester Ultrasound.

144
00:19:33,637 --> 00:19:40,237
Ultrasound should be routinely offered to all pregnant women between 18 and 22 weeks’ gestation .

145
00:19:40,237 --> 00:19:51,950
Recognizing that the gestational age at which an abnormality is identified may affect pregnancy management options, providers often opt to perform the examination before 20 weeks.

146
00:19:51,950 --> 00:20:06,712
Indications for second- and third-trimester sonograms are listed in Table 14-5 (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020; American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine, 2018a).

147
00:20:06,712 --> 00:20:16,875
Based on data from large insurance providers, pregnant women typically receive at least 4 to 5 sonograms per pregnancy (O’Keeffe, 2013).

148
00:20:16,875 --> 00:20:21,925
Examinations are classified as standard, specialized, or limited.

149
00:20:21,925 --> 00:20:38,937
Specialized examination types include the detailed fetal anatomy examination, detailed evaluation for placenta accreta spectrum (p. 253), fetal echocardiography, Doppler velocimetry (p. 262), and the biophysical profile .

150
00:20:38,937 --> 00:20:41,512
Table 14-5.

151
00:20:41,512 --> 00:20:50,200
Indications for Standard Second- and Third-trimester Ultrasound Examinations # Standard Second- and Third-Trimester Ultrasound.

152
00:20:50,200 --> 00:21:05,437
Examinations The standard examination includes evaluation of fetal number and presentation, cardiac activity, fetal biometry, amnionic fluid volume, placental location, cervical length, and a survey of fetal anatomy .

153
00:21:05,437 --> 00:21:11,462
Anatomical components are listed in Table 15-1 (p. 273).

154
00:21:11,462 --> 00:21:25,537
With twins or other multiples, documentation should also include the number of chorions and amnions, comparison of fetal sizes, estimation of amnionic fluid volume within each sac, and fetal phenotypic gender.

155
00:21:25,537 --> 00:21:37,437
The American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine (2018a) revised its prior 2013 standard ultrasound practice parameter to include the following updates: 1.

156
00:21:37,437 --> 00:21:51,075
Components added to the standard fetal anatomical survey are presence of the hands and feet, and when feasible, the three-vessel view and threevessel trachea views of the heart (Fig. 15-37, p. 290).

157
00:21:51,075 --> 00:22:00,037
2. If the relationship between placenta and cervix cannot be assessed transabdominally, transvaginal evaluation should be performed.

158
00:22:00,037 --> 00:22:05,762
Transperineal evaluation remains an option but in our experience is rarely used.

159
00:22:05,762 --> 00:22:16,137
3. If the cervix appears abnormal or is not adequately visualized transabdominally, transvaginal (or transperineal) examination is recommended.

160
00:22:16,137 --> 00:22:22,325
If cervical length assessment is requested, measurement should be based on a transvaginal image.

161
00:22:22,325 --> 00:22:34,912
4. In the setting of velamentous cord insertion, color and pulsed Doppler ultrasound should be used to evaluate for vasa previa (Figs. 6-6 and 6-7, p. 115).

162
00:22:34,912 --> 00:22:39,100
# Detailed Second- and Third-Trimester Ultrasound.

163
00:22:39,100 --> 00:22:47,987
Examinations The detailed fetal anatomy examination is also known as a targeted or 76811 examination.

164
00:22:47,987 --> 00:22:59,312
It is performed when the risk for a fetal structural or genetic abnormality is elevated because of history, screening test result, or abnormal finding during standard examination .

165
00:22:59,312 --> 00:23:08,937
The detailed ultrasound examination is intended to be indication-driven and is not repeated without an extenuating circumstance, such as a new risk factor .

166
00:23:08,937 --> 00:23:18,462
Physicians who perform or interpret these ultrasound examinations should have gained expertise in fetal imaging through both training and ongoing experience .

167
00:23:18,462 --> 00:23:21,000
Table 14-6.

168
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:34,225
American Institute for Ultrasound in Medicine Indications for Detailed Second- and Third-trimester Ultrasound Examinations Components of the detailed examination are intended to be determined on a case-by-case basis.

169
00:23:34,225 --> 00:23:42,100
Table 15-1 (p. 273) lists nearly 70 anatomical components that may be included.

170
00:23:42,100 --> 00:23:46,725
When practices apply for adjunct accreditation in performance of

171
00:23:46,876 --> 00:23:57,788
the detailed fetal anatomic survey, the American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine specifies 50 components that must be included when submitting normal cases for review.

172
00:23:57,738 --> 00:24:04,376
At Parkland Hospital, we attempt to image these components in all detailed anatomic surveys.

173
00:24:04,376 --> 00:24:14,063
When performing a given detailed examination, one challenge is determining which components are needed for a given indication, as these have not been codified.

174
00:24:14,063 --> 00:24:24,688
The most prevalent risk factors and thus indications for detailed ultrasound examinations are maternal obesity and maternal age ≥35 years.

175
00:24:24,688 --> 00:24:48,988
In a systematic review of more than 16,000 pregnancies affected by anomalies, obesity conferred modestly increased odds of a neural-tube defect (1.9), ventriculomegaly (1.7), cardiovascular anomaly (1.3), cleft lip/palate (1.2), anorectal atresia (1.5), and limb reduction defect (1.7) .

176
00:24:48,988 --> 00:24:58,713
Subsequently, Biggio and colleagues (2010) reported that obesity was associated with a higher anomaly prevalence only in the setting of diabetes.

177
00:24:58,713 --> 00:25:06,751
Maternal age ≥35 years is considered another indication for detailed sonographic examination.

178
00:25:06,751 --> 00:25:13,051
However, the fetal anomaly risk may be related to the associated increase in aneuploidy.

179
00:25:13,051 --> 00:25:21,726
Goetzinger and colleagues (2017) found that the anomaly rate among euploid fetuses born to older mothers was not increased.

180
00:25:21,726 --> 00:25:24,801
# Fetal Echocardiography.

181
00:25:24,801 --> 00:25:33,138
This specialized examination of fetal cardiac structure and function is designed to identify and characterize abnormalities.

182
00:25:33,138 --> 00:26:07,776
Echocardiography indications include suspected fetal cardiac structural or functional abnormality; heart rate abnormality or arrhythmia; extracardiac anomaly or hydrops; chromosomal abnormality; nuchal translucency ≥3.5 mm; in vitro fertilization; monochorionic twin gestation; first-degree relative to the fetus with a congenital cardiac defect; first- or second-degree relative to the fetus with a Mendelian syndrome and childhood cardiac manifestation; prior fetus with heart block in the setting

183
00:26:07,776 --> 00:26:17,438
of maternal anti-Ro or La antibodies; retinoid exposure; and metabolic risk factor such as pregestational diabetes or phenylketonuria .

184
00:26:17,438 --> 00:26:23,376
Selected cardiac anomalies are reviewed in Chap. 15 (p. 291).

185
00:26:23,376 --> 00:26:26,826
# Limited Ultrasound Examination.

186
00:26:26,826 --> 00:26:33,388
A limited second- or third-trimester examination is performed to address a specific clinical question.

187
00:26:33,388 --> 00:26:45,026
Evaluation of fetal number, presentation, and cardiac activity; amnionic fluid volume; and placental location with respect to the internal os are common indications .

188
00:26:45,026 --> 00:26:50,763
The examination may include fetal biometry but not a complete anatomical survey.

189
00:26:50,763 --> 00:26:59,238
In the absence of an emergency, a limited examination is performed only if a standard ultrasound survey has already been completed.

190
00:26:59,238 --> 00:27:07,376
Otherwise, provided that the gestational age is at least 18 weeks, a standard ultrasound examination is recommended.

191
00:27:07,376 --> 00:27:10,351
# Fetal Anomaly Detection.

192
00:27:10,351 --> 00:27:24,688
With current advances in imaging technology, approximately 60 percent of major fetal abnormalities may be detected during standard ultrasound examinations (Byrne, 2020; Rydberg, 2017).

193
00:27:24,688 --> 00:27:37,163
For detailed ultrasound surveys performed in pregnancies at increased risk for anomalies, detection rates may exceed 90 percent (Dashe, 2009; Levi, 1998).

194
00:27:37,163 --> 00:27:50,751
The sensitivity of the examination varies according to factors such as gestational age, maternal habitus, fetal position, equipment features, examination type, operator skill, and the specific abnormality.

195
00:27:50,751 --> 00:27:56,651
For example, maternal obesity lowers the anomaly detection rate by 20 percent .

196
00:27:56,651 --> 00:28:01,051
Detection rates vary considerably according to the abnormality.

197
00:28:01,051 --> 00:28:10,063
In the EUROCAT network of 28 population-based registries, 40 percent of major fetal abnormalities are detected prenatally .

198
00:28:10,063 --> 00:28:45,988
Detection rates of selected abnormalities are as follows: anencephaly, 98 percent; spina bifida, 89 percent; hydrocephaly, 82 percent; cleft lip/palate, 70 percent; hypoplastic left heart, 88 percent; transposition of the great vessels, 69 percent; diaphragmatic hernia, 73 percent; gastroschisis, 92 percent; omphalocele, 90 percent; bilateral renal agenesis, 94 percent; posterior urethral valves, 80 percent; limb reduction defects, 60 percent; and

199
00:28:46,156 --> 00:28:48,793
clubfoot, 60 percent.

200
00:28:48,743 --> 00:29:02,368
In contrast, anomalies with poor sonographic detection rates in the second trimester include microcephaly, choanal atresia, cleft palate, Hirschsprung disease, anal atresia, and congenital skin disorders.

201
00:29:02,368 --> 00:29:11,893
Although clinicians tend to focus on abnormalities amenable to sonographic detection, those that are undetectable can be no less devastating to families.

202
00:29:11,893 --> 00:29:18,093
Every sonographic examination should include a frank discussion of examination limitations.

203
00:29:18,093 --> 00:29:21,868
# Three-Dimensional Ultrasound Examination.

204
00:29:21,868 --> 00:29:30,318
Over the past three decades, three-dimensional (3-D) ultrasound has gone from a novelty to a feature of all modern equipment.

205
00:29:30,318 --> 00:29:36,656
After a region of interest is identified, a volume is acquired using a 3-D transducer.

206
00:29:36,656 --> 00:29:42,968
This volume can be rendered to display axial, sagittal, coronal, or oblique images.

207
00:29:42,968 --> 00:29:49,468
Sequential slices may be generated, similar to computed tomographic (CT) images.

208
00:29:49,468 --> 00:30:00,818
Unlike two-dimensional (2-D) scanning, which appears to be happening in real time on the screen, 3-D imaging is static and obtained by processing a volume of stored images.

209
00:30:00,818 --> 00:30:08,906
With four-dimensional ultrasound, rapid reconstruction of the rendered images conveys the impression that the scanning is in real time.

210
00:30:08,906 --> 00:30:26,468
For selected anomalies, such as those of the face and skeleton, for tumors, and for some cases of neural-tube defects, 3-D sonography can add useful information (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020; Goncalves, 2005).

211
00:30:26,468 --> 00:30:41,206
That said, comparisons of 3-D and conventional 2D sonography for the diagnosis of most congenital anomalies have not demonstrated better overall detection rates (Goncalves, 2006; Reddy, 2008).

212
00:30:41,206 --> 00:30:53,231
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) has concluded that proof of a clinical advantage of 3-D ultrasound for prenatal diagnosis is generally lacking.

213
00:30:53,231 --> 00:30:55,506
Placenta And Cervix.

214
00:30:55,506 --> 00:31:08,581
The standard examination includes evaluation of the anatomical relationship between the placenta and the internal cervical os, the umbilical cord insertion into the placenta, and cervical length assessment.

215
00:31:08,581 --> 00:31:13,956
Evaluation for placenta accreta spectrum is a detailed examination type .

216
00:31:13,956 --> 00:31:17,793
# Placenta Previa and Low-lying Placenta.

217
00:31:17,793 --> 00:31:25,456
The location of the placenta with respect to the cervix may be accurately assessed by approximately 16 weeks’ gestation.

218
00:31:25,456 --> 00:31:31,443
If transabdominal visualization is limited, transvaginal ultrasound is recommended.

219
00:31:31,443 --> 00:31:41,193
Placenta previa is diagnosed if the placenta overlies the internal cervical os to any degree or reaches its margin (Fig. 14-2A).

220
00:31:41,193 --> 00:31:52,406
If the inferior placental edge is within 2 cm of the internal os but does not reach the cervix, the diagnosis is low-lying placenta (see Fig. 14-2B).

221
00:31:52,406 --> 00:32:08,606
Whenever placenta previa or low-lying placenta are diagnosed, a follow-up ultrasound examination is recommended at approximately 32 weeks’ gestation, with transvaginal evaluation if needed to verify the relationship between the placenta and cervix.

222
00:32:08,606 --> 00:32:14,656
If findings persist, a 36-week ultrasound examination also is recommended.

223
00:32:14,656 --> 00:32:20,968
Management of these placenta types are discussed in Chapter 43 (p. 757).

224
00:32:20,968 --> 00:32:23,456
Figure 14-2.

225
00:32:23,456 --> 00:32:25,718
A. Placenta previa.

226
00:32:25,718 --> 00:32:35,456
In this transvaginal image, the inferior edge (arrow) of the posterior placenta (P) overlies the internal cervical os (arrowhead).

227
00:32:35,456 --> 00:32:37,468
H = head.

228
00:32:37,468 --> 00:32:39,993
B. Low-lying placenta.

229
00:32:39,993 --> 00:32:49,093
The inferior placental edge (arrow) is within 2 cm of the internal cervical os (arrowhead) in this transvaginal image.

230
00:32:49,093 --> 00:32:53,156
The bladder (B) is seen anterior to the cervix.

231
00:32:53,156 --> 00:32:57,818
The umbilical cord insertion into the placenta should be interrogated.

232
00:32:57,818 --> 00:33:09,268
Marginal cord insertion, also known as battledore placenta, is diagnosed if the umbilical cord inserts into the edge of the placenta or within 2 cm of the placental margin.

233
00:33:09,268 --> 00:33:20,856
If the umbilical cord does not insert into the placenta—but rather into the membranes—the diagnosis is velamentous cord insertion (Fig. 6-6, p. 115).

234
00:33:20,856 --> 00:33:30,443
With the latter, the umbilical arteries and vein traverse the membranes along the uterine wall unprotected by Wharton jelly before entering the placental margin.

235
00:33:30,443 --> 00:33:39,106
If umbilical vessels course within the portion of membrane overlying the cervix or within 2 cm of the cervix, the diagnosis is

236
00:33:39,268 --> 00:33:44,105
vasa previa (Fig. 6-7, p. 116).

237
00:33:44,055 --> 00:33:57,518
Vasa previa may also occur if there are two or more placental lobes—as with a succenturiate lobe—and the interconnecting vessels traverse the intervening membranes over or in proximity to the internal cervical os.

238
00:33:57,518 --> 00:34:08,955
Transvaginal ultrasound with color Doppler highlights the vessels, and pulsed-wave Doppler of the spanning arterial vessel demonstrates a fetal heart rate, which confirms the diagnosis.

239
00:34:08,955 --> 00:34:15,155
A subsequent detailed ultrasound examination is recommended at 32 weeks’ gestation .

240
00:34:15,155 --> 00:34:21,230
Chapter 6 (p. 114) contains content on management of these entities.

241
00:34:21,230 --> 00:34:24,255
# Placenta Accreta Spectrum.

242
00:34:24,255 --> 00:34:30,818
Placenta accreta, increta, and percreta comprise the placenta accreta spectrum (PAS).

243
00:34:30,818 --> 00:34:38,268
They are characterized by abnormal placental invasion onto, into, or through the myometrium, respectively .

244
00:34:38,268 --> 00:34:47,793
Evaluation includes transabdominal and transvaginal imaging, with and without color or power Doppler and with the patient’s bladder partially filled.

245
00:34:47,793 --> 00:35:02,680
In one metaanalysis that included more than 3200 pregnancies, the sensitivity of ultrasound to identify placenta accreta, increta, and percreta was 91, 93, and 89 percent, respectively.

246
00:35:02,680 --> 00:35:09,330
Corresponding specificities were 97, 98, and 99 percent, respectively .

247
00:35:09,330 --> 00:35:16,618
Five ultrasound criteria assist with detection and characterization of PAS : Figure 14-3.

248
00:35:16,618 --> 00:35:23,043
These third-trimester sonograms demonstrate findings that characterize placenta accreta spectrum.

249
00:35:23,043 --> 00:35:33,280
A. Transabdominal color mapping depicts bridging vessels between the bladder and the uterine serosa (arrowheads) and shows large intraplacental lacunae (arrow).

250
00:35:33,280 --> 00:35:46,230
B. Transvaginal transverse image showing a large bulge (suggesting placenta percreta) along the bladder-uterine serosal interface (arrowheads) and multiple large, irregular lacunae (arrows).

251
00:35:46,230 --> 00:35:49,980
C. Disruption of the bladderserosal interface.

252
00:35:49,980 --> 00:35:55,893
The echogenic interface between bladder and serosa appears irregular (arrowheads).

253
00:35:55,893 --> 00:36:03,455
The smallest myometrial thickness measures <1 mm, and bridging vessels are highlighted by color Doppler.

254
00:36:03,455 --> 00:36:06,855
Large lacunae also are shown (arrows).

255
00:36:06,855 --> 00:36:13,855
1. Placental lacunae, which are vascular spaces that may contain prominent color Doppler flow 2.

256
00:36:13,855 --> 00:36:22,655
Attenuation or thinning of the retroplacental myometrium, such that the smallest myometrial thickness measurement is <1 mm.

257
00:36:22,655 --> 00:36:27,480
This is also referred to as loss of the retroplacental clear space 3.

258
00:36:27,480 --> 00:36:38,055
Disruption of the bladder-uterine serosal interface, which appears as an irregular, echogenic boundary between the bladder and uterine serosa with gray-scale imaging 4.

259
00:36:38,055 --> 00:36:45,543
Bridging vessels, which are demonstrated with color Doppler to course from the placenta to the bladder-serosal interface 5.

260
00:36:45,543 --> 00:36:51,605
A placental “bulge” that pushes outward and distorts the contour of the uterus or other organs.

261
00:36:51,605 --> 00:36:57,193
In some cases of placenta percreta, a focal exophytic mass also is seen.

262
00:36:57,193 --> 00:37:14,593
The accuracy of these criteria varies in published series and is affected by the number and predictive value of given findings and by associated risk factors such as placenta previa and number of prior cesarean deliveries (Jauniaux, 2016; Rac, 2015).

263
00:37:14,593 --> 00:37:19,905
Serial evaluation may be helpful, particularly in the third trimester.

264
00:37:19,905 --> 00:37:28,880
With concurrent placenta previa and history of cesarean delivery, any of these ultrasound findings prompts a detailed ultrasound evaluation.

265
00:37:28,880 --> 00:37:38,043
The role of magnetic resonance (MR) imaging as an adjunct in pregnancies with suspected PAS is reviewed later (p. 268).

266
00:37:38,043 --> 00:37:41,168
# Cesarean Scar Pregnancy.

267
00:37:41,168 --> 00:37:57,393
Placental implantation within a prior hysterotomy scar—termed cesarean scar pregnancy (CSP)—is often a precursor to second- or third-trimester PAS (Happe, 2020; Rac, 2016; Timor-Trisch, 2014).

268
00:37:57,393 --> 00:38:03,143
Sonographically, the gestational sac lies low and anteriorly in the uterus.

269
00:38:03,143 --> 00:38:12,118
CSPs may appear to rest on the prior scar or may fill the niche, which is a myometrial pocket defect in the thinned hysterotomy scar .

270
00:38:12,118 --> 00:38:17,430
Placental sonolucencies, a precursor to PAS lacunae, may be seen.

271
00:38:17,430 --> 00:38:33,905
The retroplacental myometrium may be attenuated to a degree that the distance from the anterior trophoblastic border to the uterine serosa measures <3 mm (D’Antonio, 2018; Moschos, 2014; Happe, 2020).

272
00:38:34,060 --> 00:38:40,422
Color Doppler may demonstrate prominent vascularity in the region of the prior hysterotomy scar .

273
00:38:40,372 --> 00:38:45,047
In some cases, the gestational sac may bulge toward the bladder.

274
00:38:45,047 --> 00:38:51,272
CSP evaluation and management are discussed in Chapter 12 (p. 229).

275
00:38:51,272 --> 00:38:53,835
Figure 14-4.

276
00:38:53,835 --> 00:38:58,072
Cesarean scar pregnancy, transvaginal images.

277
00:38:58,072 --> 00:39:02,497
A. The trophoblast appears to fill the scar “niche” (arrows).

278
00:39:02,497 --> 00:39:07,510
B. Outward bulging of the gestational sac toward the bladder (arrowheads).

279
00:39:07,510 --> 00:39:11,222
C. Placental sonolucencies (arrows).

280
00:39:11,222 --> 00:39:15,260
D. Attenuation of the retroplacental myometrium.

281
00:39:15,260 --> 00:39:22,385
The distance from the anterior trophoblastic border to the uterine serosa is <1 mm (arrows).

282
00:39:22,385 --> 00:39:28,110
Color Doppler demonstrates vascularity in the region of the prior hysterotomy scar.

283
00:39:28,110 --> 00:39:30,560
# Cervical Length.

284
00:39:30,560 --> 00:39:43,322
Although the cervix may be imaged transabdominally (Fig. 14-5A), visualization is often limited by maternal habitus, cervical position, or shadowing by the fetal presenting part.

285
00:39:43,322 --> 00:39:50,597
In addition, the maternal bladder or pressure from the transducer may artificially elongate the cervix’s appearance.

286
00:39:50,597 --> 00:39:57,922
As a result, values from transabdominal or transvaginal measurement of the cervix can differ significantly.

287
00:39:57,922 --> 00:40:00,535
Figure 14-5.

288
00:40:00,535 --> 00:40:06,310
A. Transabdominal image of the cervix depicting the internal os and external os.

289
00:40:06,310 --> 00:40:14,110
B. Transvaginal imaging provides a more accurate evaluation of the cervix and should be used for medical decision-making.

290
00:40:14,110 --> 00:40:18,285
In this image, arrowheads mark the endocervical canal.

291
00:40:18,285 --> 00:40:22,335
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Emily Adhikari.

292
00:40:22,335 --> 00:40:31,047
If the cervix appears short or is inadequately visualized during transabdominal evaluation, transvaginal assessment should be considered .

293
00:40:31,047 --> 00:40:38,885
Clinical decisionmaking should use only cervical length measurements obtained transvaginally (Fig. 14-5B).

294
00:40:38,885 --> 00:40:43,635
Measurement should be performed at or beyond 16 weeks’ gestation.

295
00:40:43,635 --> 00:40:51,610
A short cervix is associated with an elevated risk for preterm birth, particularly in those with prior preterm birth.

296
00:40:51,610 --> 00:40:55,872
Risk rises proportionally with the degree of cervical shortening .

297
00:40:55,872 --> 00:41:03,097
To measure the cervix transvaginally, the imaging criteria shown in Table 14-7 are followed.

298
00:41:03,097 --> 00:41:11,897
The endocervical canal should be visible in its entirety, and images ideally are obtained over several minutes to capture dynamic change.

299
00:41:11,897 --> 00:41:18,722
Funneling is a protrusion of amnionic membranes into a portion of the endocervical canal that has dilated .

300
00:41:18,722 --> 00:41:24,947
Funneling is not an independent predictor of preterm birth but is associated with cervical shortening.

301
00:41:24,947 --> 00:41:30,410
Transvaginal assessment is recommended if a funnel is suspected transabdominally.

302
00:41:30,410 --> 00:41:37,360
The cervical length is measured distal to the funnel, because the funnel’s base becomes the functional internal os.

303
00:41:37,360 --> 00:41:47,922
If the cervix is dilated, as with cervical insufficiency, the membranes may prolapse through the endocervical canal and into the vagina to produce an hourglass appearance.

304
00:41:47,922 --> 00:41:55,772
Sludge represents an aggregate of particulate matter (debris) within the amnionic sac and close to the internal os.

305
00:41:55,772 --> 00:42:01,047
In pregnancies at risk for preterm birth, sludge further raises the risk.

306
00:42:01,047 --> 00:42:03,560
Table 14-7.

307
00:42:03,560 --> 00:42:09,397
Criteria for Transvaginal Evaluation of the Cervix Figure 14-6.

308
00:42:09,397 --> 00:42:13,947
Transvaginal image depicting a foreshortened cervix with funneling.

309
00:42:13,947 --> 00:42:20,772
Funneling is a protrusion of amnionic membranes into a portion of the endocervical canal that has dilated.

310
00:42:20,772 --> 00:42:26,610
The distal protruding edge of the funnel becomes the functional internal os (left arrow).

311
00:42:26,610 --> 00:42:32,722
Thus, the measured cervical length, which lies between the arrows, should not include the funnel.

312
00:42:32,722 --> 00:42:36,772
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Emily Adhikari.

313
00:42:36,772 --> 00:42:38,810
Amnionic Fluid.

314
00:42:38,810 --> 00:42:41,222
# Physiology.

315
00:42:41,222 --> 00:42:44,410
Amnionic fluid serves several roles.

316
00:42:44,410 --> 00:42:52,660
Fetal breathing is essential for normal lung growth, and fetal swallowing permits gastrointestinal (GI) tract development.

317
00:42:52,660 --> 00:43:00,372
The fluid also creates a physical space for fetal movement, which is necessary for neuromusculoskeletal maturation.

318
00:43:00,372 --> 00:43:06,947
Amnionic fluid further guards against umbilical cord compression and protects the fetus from trauma.

319
00:43:06,947 --> 00:43:10,360
It also has bacteriostatic properties.

320
00:43:10,360 --> 00:43:16,522
Early in pregnancy, amnionic fluid is similar in composition to extracellular fluid.

321
00:43:16,522 --> 00:43:24,085
Amnionic fluid arises as a transudate of plasma either from the fetus through its nonkeratinized skin or from the mother

322
00:43:24,244 --> 00:43:27,981
across the uterine decidua and placenta surface .

323
00:43:27,931 --> 00:43:44,606
Specifically, early transfer of water and other small molecules occurs via three mechanisms: across the amnion—transmembranous flow; across fetal vessels on the placental surface—intramembranous flow; and across fetal skin— transcutaneous flow.

324
00:43:44,606 --> 00:44:00,406
Fetal urine production does not become a major component of amnionic fluid until the second trimester, which explains why fetuses with lethal renal abnormalities may not manifest severe amnionic volume declines until after 18 weeks’ gestation.

325
00:44:00,406 --> 00:44:07,694
Water transport across the fetal skin continues until keratinization occurs at 22 to 25 weeks.

326
00:44:07,694 --> 00:44:14,019
In the second half of pregnancy, fetal urination is the primary source of amnionic fluid.

327
00:44:14,019 --> 00:44:22,431
In late gestation, the fetal respiratory tract also produces approximately 350 mL of lung fluid per day.

328
00:44:22,431 --> 00:44:30,894
Fetal swallowing is the primary mechanism for amnionic fluid resorption and averages 500 to 1000 mL per day .

329
00:44:30,894 --> 00:44:36,819
By term, the entire amnionic fluid volume is recirculated on a daily basis .

330
00:44:36,819 --> 00:44:49,381
However, impaired swallowing, secondary to either a central nervous system (CNS) abnormality or GI tract obstruction, can result in impressive amnionic fluid volume expansion.

331
00:44:49,381 --> 00:44:51,794
Table 14-8.

332
00:44:51,794 --> 00:45:02,794
Amnionic Fluid Volume Regulation in Late Pregnancy The osmolality of amnionic fluid is similar to that of fetal urine and hypotonic to that of maternal and fetal plasma.

333
00:45:02,794 --> 00:45:14,744
Specifically, the osmolality of maternal and fetal plasma approximates 280 mOsm/mL, whereas that of amnionic fluid is about 260 mOsm/L.

334
00:45:14,744 --> 00:45:25,369
The hypotonicity of amnionic fluid accounts for up to 400 mL per day of intramembranous fluid transfer across and into fetal vessels on the placental surface .

335
00:45:25,369 --> 00:45:36,069
Maternal dehydration can lead to higher maternal osmolality, which favors fluid transfer from the fetus to mother and then from the amnionic fluid compartment into the fetus .

336
00:45:36,069 --> 00:45:52,369
Amnionic fluid volume expands from approximately 30 mL at 10 weeks’ gestation to 200 mL by 16 weeks and reaches 800 mL by the mid-third trimester (Brace, 1989; Magann, 1997).

337
00:45:52,369 --> 00:46:11,181
Using dye dilution, Magann and associates (1997) reported that the average amnionic fluid volume was approximately 400 mL between 22 and 30 weeks’ gestation, then rose to 800 mL until 40 weeks, and subsequently declined by 8 percent per week.

338
00:46:11,181 --> 00:46:15,856
There was a wide normal range, particularly in the third trimester.

339
00:46:15,856 --> 00:46:25,994
Abnormally decreased fluid volume is termed oligohydramnios, whereas abnormally increased fluid volume is termed hydramnios or polyhydramnios.

340
00:46:25,994 --> 00:46:29,119
# Semi-quantitative Assessment.

341
00:46:29,119 --> 00:46:36,644
Evaluation of amnionic fluid volume is a component of every second- or third-trimester ultrasound examination.

342
00:46:36,644 --> 00:46:44,606
Volume is measured semiquantitatively using the single deepest pocket of fluid or the amnionic fluid index (AFI).

343
00:46:44,606 --> 00:46:55,344
Both measurements are reproducible and, in the setting of a fluid abnormality, can be followed serially over time to assess trends and aid communication among providers.

344
00:46:55,344 --> 00:46:59,894
For this reason, subjective assessment alone is not recommended.

345
00:46:59,894 --> 00:47:09,769
The single deepest pocket of fluid is measured in a sagittal plane with the ultrasound transducer held perpendicular to the floor and parallel to the long axis of the woman.

346
00:47:09,769 --> 00:47:18,844
A pocket should be at least 1 cm wide to be considered adequate, and the measurement should not include fetal parts or loops of umbilical cord.

347
00:47:18,844 --> 00:47:24,656
Color Doppler is generally used to verify that umbilical cord is not within the measurement.

348
00:47:24,656 --> 00:47:36,894
The measurement is considered normal if it is >2 cm and <8 cm. Values below and above this range indicating oligohydramnios and hydramnios, respectively.

349
00:47:36,894 --> 00:47:41,719
These thresholds correspond to the 3rd and 97th percentiles .

350
00:47:41,719 --> 00:47:57,169
When evaluating twins and other multifetal gestations, a single deepest pocket is assessed in each gestational sac, using the same normal range (Hernandez, 2012; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2013).

351
00:47:57,169 --> 00:48:07,206
The fetal biophysical profile similarly uses a single deepest vertical pocket threshold of >2 cm to indicate normal amnionic fluid volume .

352
00:48:07,206 --> 00:48:16,019
To measure the AFI, the uterus is divided into four equal quadrants—the right and left upper and lower quadrants, respectively.

353
00:48:16,019 --> 00:48:17,344
The

354
00:48:17,500 --> 00:48:21,962
AFI is the sum of the single deepest pocket from each quadrant.

355
00:48:21,912 --> 00:48:33,287
In a study of 1400 measurements obtained from the INTERGROWTH-21st trial, the mean intraand interobserver variability of AFI measurements were each below 1 cm .

356
00:48:33,287 --> 00:48:39,900
Two standard deviations from the mean, however, reached 5 and 7 cm, respectively.

357
00:48:39,900 --> 00:48:47,025
As useful rule of thumb, the AFI is generally three times higher than the single deepest pocket of fluid.

358
00:48:47,025 --> 00:48:54,425
The AFI is considered normal if >5 cm and <24 or 25 cm.

359
00:48:54,425 --> 00:49:08,375
Using an AFI nomogram based on cross-sectional evaluation of nearly 800 uncomplicated pregnancies, the mean AFI ranged between 12 and 15 cm from 16 weeks until 40 weeks’ gestation .

360
00:49:08,375 --> 00:49:16,987
Other investigators have published nomograms with similar mean values (Hinh, 2005; Machado, 2007).

361
00:49:16,987 --> 00:49:19,525
Figure 14-7.

362
00:49:19,525 --> 00:49:26,462
Amnionic fluid index (AFI) according to gestational age–specific and threshold values.

363
00:49:26,462 --> 00:49:37,937
The blue curves represent the 2.5th, 50th, and 97.5th AFI percentile values, based on the nomogram by Moore (1990).

364
00:49:37,937 --> 00:49:48,925
Red and tan curves represent 50th percentile values for AFI from Machado (2007) and from Hinh and Ladinsky (2005), respectively.

365
00:49:48,925 --> 00:49:57,375
The light blue and yellow shaded bars indicate threshold values used to define hydramnios and oligohydramnios, respectively.

366
00:49:57,375 --> 00:49:59,812
# Hydramnios.

367
00:49:59,812 --> 00:50:12,025
Abnormally increased amnionic fluid volume complicates 1 to 2 percent of singleton pregnancies (Dashe, 2002; Khan, 2017; Pri-Paz, 2012).

368
00:50:12,025 --> 00:50:30,637
Hydramnios may be categorized as mild if the AFI is 25 to 29.9 cm; moderate, if 30 to 34.9 cm; and severe, if ≥35 cm (Luo, 2017; Odibo, 2016).

369
00:50:30,637 --> 00:50:48,612
Using a single deepest pocket of amnionic fluid, as is done in multifetal gestations, mild hydramnios is defined as 8 to 9.9 cm, moderate as 10 to 11.9 cm, and severe hydramnios as ≥12 cm .

370
00:50:48,612 --> 00:50:55,512
Mild hydramnios accounts for approximately two thirds of cases and is frequently idiopathic and benign.

371
00:50:55,512 --> 00:51:04,037
By comparison, severe hydramnios is far more likely to have an underlying etiology and to have consequences for the pregnancy.

372
00:51:04,037 --> 00:51:06,450
Figure 14-8.

373
00:51:06,450 --> 00:51:08,812
Severe hydramnios.

374
00:51:08,812 --> 00:51:17,775
This pocket of amnionic fluid measured >15 cm, and the amnionic fluid index measured nearly 50 cm.

375
00:51:17,775 --> 00:51:28,612
Underlying causes of hydramnios include fetal structural abnormalities or genetic syndromes in approximately 15 percent and diabetes in 15 to 20 percent .

376
00:51:28,612 --> 00:51:35,775
Selected anomalies and the likely mechanism by which they cause hydramnios are shown in Table 14-10.

377
00:51:35,775 --> 00:51:43,912
Congenital infection, red blood cell alloimmunization, and placental chorioangioma are less frequent etiologies.

378
00:51:43,912 --> 00:51:52,687
Hydramnios may also complicate syphilis and cytomegalovirus, toxoplasmosis, and parvovirus infections (Chaps.

379
00:51:52,687 --> 00:51:58,262
67 and 68, pp. 1183 and 1206).

380
00:51:58,262 --> 00:52:02,187
Hydramnios is often seen with hydrops fetalis .

381
00:52:02,187 --> 00:52:11,900
The underlying pathophysiology in such cases is frequently related to a highcardiac-output state, and severe fetal anemia a classic example.

382
00:52:11,900 --> 00:52:17,812
A detailed ultrasound examination is indicated whenever hydramnios is identified.

383
00:52:17,812 --> 00:52:24,725
If a fetal abnormality is identified at that time, the aneuploidy risk is significantly elevated.

384
00:52:24,725 --> 00:52:27,200
Table 14-9.

385
00:52:27,200 --> 00:52:34,362
Hydramnios: Prevalence and Associated Etiologies—Values in Percent Table 14-10.

386
00:52:34,362 --> 00:52:42,875
Selected Anomalies and Mechanism for Hydramnios The degree of hydramnios positively correlates with the likelihood of an anomalous fetus.

387
00:52:42,875 --> 00:52:55,550
At Parkland Hospital, the prevalence of an anomalous neonate approximated 8 percent with mild hydramnios, 12 percent with moderate hydramnios, and more than 30 percent with severe hydramnios .

388
00:52:55,550 --> 00:53:10,000
Even if no abnormality was detected during targeted sonographic evaluation, the likelihood of a major anomaly identified at birth was 1 to 2 percent if hydramnios was mild or moderate and 10 percent if hydramnios was severe.

389
00:53:10,000 --> 00:53:25,587
The overall reported risk that an underlying anomaly will be discovered after delivery ranges from 9 percent in the neonatal period to 28 percent among infants followed to 1 year of age (Abele, 2012; Dorleijn, 2009).

390
00:53:25,587 --> 00:53:43,550
The amnionic fluid glucose concentration is higher in diabetic women than in those without diabetes, and the AFI may correlate positively with the amnionic fluid glucose concentration (Dashe, 2000; Spellacy, 1973; Weiss, 1985).

391
00:53:43,550 --> 00:53:46,712
Such findings support the hypothesis

392
00:53:46,876 --> 00:53:56,163
that maternal hyperglycemia causes fetal hyperglycemia, which leads to fetal osmotic diuresis into the amnionic fluid compartment.

393
00:53:56,113 --> 00:54:06,226
Repeat screening for gestational diabetes in pregnancies with hydramnios does not appear to be beneficial if the secondtrimester glucose tolerance test result was normal .

394
00:54:06,226 --> 00:54:12,063
Hydramnios is more frequently noted in multifetal gestations than in singleton ones.

395
00:54:12,063 --> 00:54:24,863
In a review of nearly 2000 twin gestations, Hernandez and coworkers (2012) identified hydramnios in 18 percent of both monochorionic and dichorionic pregnancies.

396
00:54:24,863 --> 00:54:36,088
In monochorionic gestations, hydramnios in one sac and oligohydramnios in the other are diagnostic criteria for twin-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS) .

397
00:54:36,088 --> 00:54:41,888
Isolated hydramnios solely in one sac also may precede this syndrome’s development .

398
00:54:41,888 --> 00:54:48,763
In the absence of a fetal abnormality or TTTS, complication risks are not generally higher .

399
00:54:48,763 --> 00:54:52,876
Idiopathic hydramnios is a diagnosis of exclusion.

400
00:54:52,876 --> 00:55:07,951
It accounts for up to 70 percent of all hydramnios cases, is mild in approximately 80 percent of idiopathic cases, and subsequently resolves in more than one third (Odibo, 2016; Wiegand, 2016).

401
00:55:07,951 --> 00:55:15,313
Management Severe hydramnios occasionally results in early preterm labor or maternal respiratory compromise.

402
00:55:15,313 --> 00:55:21,701
In such cases, large-volume amniocentesis—termed amnioreduction—may be needed.

403
00:55:21,701 --> 00:55:29,163
The technique is similar to that for genetic amniocentesis but is generally done with an 18- or 20-gauge needle.

404
00:55:29,163 --> 00:55:34,276
Fluid is collected in either a vacuum container bottle or a larger syringe .

405
00:55:34,276 --> 00:55:44,926
Approximately 1000 to 2000 mL of fluid is slowly withdrawn over 20 to 30 minutes, depending on the severity of hydramnios and gestational age.

406
00:55:44,926 --> 00:55:49,926
The goal is to restore amnionic fluid volume to the upper normal range.

407
00:55:49,926 --> 00:55:55,863
Subsequent amnioreduction procedures may be required as often as weekly or semiweekly.

408
00:55:55,863 --> 00:56:14,001
In a review of 138 singleton pregnancies requiring amnioreduction for hydramnios, a fetal GI malformation was identified in 20 percent, a chromosomal abnormality or genetic condition in almost 30 percent, and a neurological abnormality in 8 percent .

409
00:56:14,001 --> 00:56:18,788
In only 20 percent of cases was the hydramnios idiopathic.

410
00:56:18,788 --> 00:56:28,038
The initial amnioreduction procedure was performed at 31 weeks’ gestation, and the median gestational age at delivery was 36 weeks.

411
00:56:28,038 --> 00:56:37,301
Outcomes Hydramnios can be associated with preterm birth, placental abruption, uterine dysfunction during labor, and postpartum hemorrhage.

412
00:56:37,301 --> 00:56:49,038
When an underlying cause is identified, the severity of hydramnios positively correlates with risk for preterm delivery, small-for-gestational age newborn, and perinatal mortality .

413
00:56:49,038 --> 00:57:00,801
However, idiopathic hydramnios is generally not associated with preterm birth (Magann, 2010; Many, 1995; Panting-Kemp, 1999).

414
00:57:00,801 --> 00:57:11,963
Placental abruption may result from rapid decompression of an overdistended uterus following rupture of membranes or therapeutic amnioreduction, occasionally days or weeks later.

415
00:57:11,963 --> 00:57:18,801
Uterine dysfunction from overdistention may lead to postpartum atony and associated postpartum hemorrhage.

416
00:57:18,801 --> 00:57:36,038
With idiopathic hydramnios, birthweight exceeds 4000 g in nearly 25 percent of cases, and the likelihood is greater if the hydramnios is moderate or severe (Luo, 2017; Odibo, 2016; Wiegand, 2016).

417
00:57:36,038 --> 00:57:48,913
A rationale for this association is that larger fetuses have higher urine output, by virtue of their increased volume of distribution, and fetal urine is the largest contributor to amnionic fluid volume.

418
00:57:48,913 --> 00:58:04,513
Cesarean delivery rates are also higher in pregnancies with idiopathic hydramnios, and reported rates range from 35 to 55 percent (Dorleijn, 2009; Khan, 2017; Odibo, 2016).

419
00:58:04,513 --> 00:58:16,626
An unresolved question is whether hydramnios alone raises the risk for perinatal mortality (Khan, 2017; Pilliod, 2015; Wiegand, 2016).

420
00:58:16,626 --> 00:58:29,751
Using birth certificate data from California, Pilliod and colleagues (2015) reported that at 37 weeks’ gestation, the stillbirth risk was sevenfold higher in pregnancies with hydramnios.

421
00:58:29,751 --> 00:58:35,551
Risks appear to be compounded when fetal-growth restriction is comorbid with hydramnios .

422
00:58:35,551 --> 00:58:38,338
# Oligohydramnios.

423
00:58:38,338 --> 00:58:45,976
Abnormally decreased amnionic fluid volume complicates 1 to 2 percent of pregnancies (Casey, 2000;

424
00:58:46,132 --> 00:58:49,082
Petrozella, 2011).

425
00:58:49,032 --> 00:58:57,894
Oligohydramnios is diagnosed if the AFI measures <5 cm or the single deepest pocket is <2 cm .

426
00:58:57,894 --> 00:59:07,894
An AFI threshold of 5 cm is below the 2.5th percentile throughout the second and third trimesters (see Fig. 14-7).

427
00:59:07,894 --> 00:59:22,669
Importantly, use of AFI rather than single deepest pocket will identify more pregnancies as having oligohydramnios but without evidence of improved pregnancy outcomes (Kehl, 2016; Nabhan, 2010).

428
00:59:22,669 --> 00:59:32,257
When evaluating multifetal pregnancies for TTTS, a single deepest pocket <2 cm is used to define oligohydramnios .

429
00:59:32,257 --> 00:59:38,744
When no measurable pocket of amnionic fluid is identified, the term anhydramnios is used.

430
00:59:38,744 --> 00:59:45,382
By 18 weeks’ gestation, the fetal kidneys are the main contributor to amnionic fluid volume.

431
00:59:45,382 --> 01:00:04,844
Selected renal abnormalities that lead to absent fetal urine production include bilateral renal agenesis, bilateral multicystic dysplastic kidney, unilateral renal agenesis with contralateral multicystic dysplastic kidney, and the infantile form of autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease.

432
01:00:04,844 --> 01:00:17,094
Lower urinary abnormalities may also cause oligohydramnios because of fetal bladder outlet obstruction (Figs. 15-58 through 15-61, p. 300).

433
01:00:17,094 --> 01:00:26,257
Complex fetal genitourinary abnormalities such as persistent cloaca and sirenomelia similarly may result in a lack of amnionic fluid.

434
01:00:26,257 --> 01:00:36,282
With oligohydramnios before the mid-second trimester, particularly before 20 to 22 weeks, pulmonary hypoplasia is a significant concern.

435
01:00:36,282 --> 01:00:40,994
The prognosis is extremely poor unless fetal therapy is an option .

436
01:00:40,994 --> 01:00:47,869
Oligohydramnios is also associated with exposure to drugs that block the renin-angiotensin system.

437
01:00:47,869 --> 01:00:58,769
These include angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors, angiotensin-receptor blockers, and nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).

438
01:00:58,769 --> 01:01:16,794
When taken in the second or third trimester, ACE inhibitors and angiotensin-receptor blockers may result in fetal hypotension, renal hypoperfusion, and renal ischemia, leading to subsequent anuric renal failure (Bullo, 2012; Guron, 2000).

439
01:01:16,794 --> 01:01:21,944
Fetal skull bone hypoplasia and limb contractures also have been described .

440
01:01:21,944 --> 01:01:29,669
Additionally, NSAIDs can be associated with fetal ductus arteriosus constriction and impaired fetal urine production .

441
01:01:29,669 --> 01:01:37,944
Oligohydramnios in the late second trimester or in the third trimester is often associated with uteroplacental insufficiency.

442
01:01:37,944 --> 01:01:45,319
A placental abnormality or a maternal complication such as preeclampsia or vascular disease are examples.

443
01:01:45,319 --> 01:01:49,007
Initially, ruptured membranes should be excluded.

444
01:01:49,007 --> 01:01:58,619
Then, particularly in the second trimester, a detailed ultrasound examination should be performed to search for fetal and placental abnormalities.

445
01:01:58,619 --> 01:02:12,132
If a placental hematoma or chronic abruption is sufficiently severe to result in oligohydramnios—the chronic abruption-oligohydramnios sequence (CAOS) —then it commonly also causes growth restriction .

446
01:02:12,132 --> 01:02:23,619
Management Oligohydramnios detected before 36 weeks’ gestation in the presence of normal fetal anatomy and growth is generally managed expectantly and coupled with fetal surveillance .

447
01:02:23,619 --> 01:02:31,819
For late-preterm and early-term pregnancies, risks of fetal compromise outweigh potential complications of preterm delivery.

448
01:02:31,819 --> 01:02:42,882
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021b) recommends delivery between 360/7 and 376/7 weeks.

449
01:02:42,882 --> 01:02:54,007
In a review of 16 trials of pregnancies with apparent isolated oligohydramnios, oral or intravenous hydration was associated with a significantly improved AFI.

450
01:02:54,007 --> 01:02:59,394
However, it was unclear whether this translated into better pregnancy outcomes .

451
01:02:59,394 --> 01:03:18,782
Outcomes In a review of pregnancies with oligohydramnios at Parkland Hospital, Petrozella and associates (2011) found that an AFI <5 cm identified between 24 and 34 weeks’ gestation was associated with increased risks for perinatal morbidity and mortality .

452
01:03:18,782 --> 01:03:37,032
Similarly, a metaanalysis comprising more than 10,000 pregnancies found that oligohydramnios conferred a twofold risk for cesarean delivery due to nonreassuring fetal status and a fivefold risk for an Apgar score <7 at 5 minutes compared with pregnancies with a normal AFI .

453
01:03:37,032 --> 01:03:39,607
Table 14-11.

454
01:03:39,607 --> 01:03:43,044
Pregnancy Outcomes in Women Diagnosed with

455
01:03:43,204 --> 01:04:00,416
Oligohydramnios between 24 and 34 Weeks’ Gestation at Parkland Hospital As discussed, if oligohydramnios is defined as an AFI <5 cm rather than a single deepest pocket <2 cm, more pregnancies will be classified as such.

456
01:04:00,416 --> 01:04:17,241
Kehl and coworkers (2016) performed a prospective trial with more than 1000 term pregnancies in which women with an AFI <5 cm or a single deepest pocket <2 cm were randomly assigned to labor induction or expectant care.

457
01:04:17,241 --> 01:04:28,591
Significantly more pregnancies were diagnosed with oligohydramnios using the AFI criterion—10 percent compared with just 2 percent—when single deepest pocket was used.

458
01:04:28,591 --> 01:04:35,479
This led to a higher rate of labor induction in the AFI group but not to a difference in neonatal outcomes.

459
01:04:35,479 --> 01:04:40,566
Borderline Oligohydramnios This diagnosis is somewhat controversial.

460
01:04:40,566 --> 01:04:43,504
Also called borderline AFI.

461
01:04:43,504 --> 01:04:52,354
It usually refers to an AFI between 5 and 8 cm (Magann, 2011; Petrozella, 2011).

462
01:04:52,354 --> 01:05:02,154
During the mid-third trimester, an AFI value of 8 cm is below the 5th percentile on the Moore nomogram (see Fig. 14-7).

463
01:05:02,154 --> 01:05:21,779
Petrozella (2011) found that pregnancies between 24 and 34 weeks’ gestation with an AFI between 5 and 8 cm were not more likely than those with an AFI >8 cm to have maternal hypertensive complications or an increased risk for stillbirth and neonatal death.

464
01:05:21,779 --> 01:05:34,754
Wood and colleagues (2014) reported a higher rate of fetalgrowth restriction in pregnancies with borderline AFI but not an increase in rates of preterm delivery or need for neonatal intensive care.

465
01:05:34,754 --> 01:05:50,066
In a study of late-preterm pregnancies that were otherwise uncomplicated, borderline AFI conferred no increased risk for preterm delivery, nonreassuring fetal heart rate tracing, low Apgar score, or neonatal respiratory compromise .

466
01:05:50,066 --> 01:05:55,316
Evidence is insufficient to support fetal surveillance or delivery in this setting .

467
01:05:55,316 --> 01:05:56,866
Doppler.

468
01:05:56,866 --> 01:06:08,691
When sound waves strike a moving target, the frequency of the waves reflected back is shifted in proportion to the velocity and direction of that moving target—a phenomenon known as the Doppler shift.

469
01:06:08,691 --> 01:06:17,691
Because magnitude and direction of the frequency shift depend on the motion of a moving target, Doppler can help evaluate flow within blood vessels.

470
01:06:17,691 --> 01:06:24,679
An important component of the Doppler equation is the angle of insonation, abbreviated as theta (θ) .

471
01:06:24,679 --> 01:06:29,966
This is the angle between the sound waves from the transducer and flow within the vessel.

472
01:06:29,966 --> 01:06:39,641
Measurement error becomes large when θ is not close to zero, in other words, when blood flow is not coming directly toward or away from the transducer.

473
01:06:39,641 --> 01:06:48,104
For this reason, ratios are often used to compare different waveform components and allow cosine θ to cancel out of the equation.

474
01:06:48,104 --> 01:06:55,241
Figure 14-10 is a schematic of the Doppler waveform and describes the three ratios commonly used.

475
01:06:55,241 --> 01:07:07,854
The simplest is the systolic-diastolic ratio (S/D ratio), which compares the maximal (or peak) systolic flow with end-diastolic flow to evaluate downstream impedance to flow.

476
01:07:07,854 --> 01:07:13,104
Currently, two types of Doppler modalities are available for clinical use.

477
01:07:13,104 --> 01:07:15,666
Figure 14-9.

478
01:07:15,666 --> 01:07:17,666
Doppler equation.

479
01:07:17,666 --> 01:07:29,829
Ultrasound emanating from the transducer with initial frequency f o strikes blood moving at velocity v. Reflected frequency f d is dependent on angle θ between beam of sound and vessel.

480
01:07:29,829 --> 01:07:32,304
Figure 14-10.

481
01:07:32,304 --> 01:07:37,529
Doppler systolic–diastolic waveform indices of blood flow velocity.

482
01:07:37,529 --> 01:07:45,216
S represents the peak systolic flow or velocity, and D indicates the end-diastolic flow or velocity.

483
01:07:45,216 --> 01:07:51,916
The mean, which is the time-average mean velocity, is calculated from computer-digitized waveforms.

484
01:07:51,916 --> 01:08:01,804
Continuous-wave Doppler equipment contains two types of crystals, one to transmit high-frequency sound waves and another to continuously capture signals.

485
01:08:01,804 --> 01:08:10,691
In M-mode imaging, continuous-wave Doppler is used to evaluate motion through time, however, it cannot image individual vessels.

486
01:08:10,691 --> 01:08:20,404
Pulsed-wave Doppler uses only one crystal, which transmits the signal and then waits until the returning signal is received before transmitting another one.

487
01:08:20,404 --> 01:08:25,741
It allows precise visualization and color-flow mapping of the vessel of interest.

488
01:08:25,741 --> 01:08:30,704
By convention, blood flowing toward the transducer is displayed in red

489
01:08:30,868 --> 01:08:34,830
and that flowing away from the transducer appears in blue.

490
01:08:34,780 --> 01:08:37,355
# Umbilical Artery.

491
01:08:37,355 --> 01:08:44,668
The umbilical artery differs from other vessels in that it normally has forward flow throughout the entire cardiac cycle.

492
01:08:44,668 --> 01:08:52,018
With advancing gestation, the amount of flow during diastole increases because of decreasing placental impedance.

493
01:08:52,018 --> 01:09:04,055
The S/D ratio approximates ≤4.0 after 20 weeks’ gestation, <3.0 after 30 weeks’, and close to 2.0 at term.

494
01:09:04,055 --> 01:09:13,130
More end-diastolic flow is observed at the placental cord insertion than at the fetal ventral wall, a reflection of downstream impedance to flow.

495
01:09:13,130 --> 01:09:21,568
Thus, abnormalities such as absent or reversed end-diastolic flow will appear first at the cord insertion site into the fetus.

496
01:09:21,568 --> 01:09:31,130
The International Society of Ultrasound in Obstetrics and Gynecology recommends that umbilical artery Doppler measurements be made in a free loop of cord .

497
01:09:31,130 --> 01:09:39,693
However, assessment close to the ventral wall insertion may optimize measurement reproducibility in cases in which flow is diminished .

498
01:09:39,693 --> 01:09:47,280
The waveform is considered abnormal if the S/D ratio is >95th percentile for gestational age.

499
01:09:47,280 --> 01:09:52,068
Threshold values are listed in the Appendix (p. 1242).

500
01:09:52,068 --> 01:10:01,105
In extreme cases of growth restriction, end-diastolic flow can become absent or even reversed (Fig. 47, p. 830).

501
01:10:01,105 --> 01:10:15,368
Such reversal of enddiastolic flow has been associated with greater than 70-percent obliteration of the small muscular arteries in placental tertiary stem villi (Kingdom, 1997; Morrow, 1989).

502
01:10:15,368 --> 01:10:21,080
Umbilical artery Doppler has been rigorously investigated to test of fetal well-being.

503
01:10:21,080 --> 01:10:31,180
As described in Chapter 47 (p. 830), this tool aids management of fetal-growth restriction and is associated with improved outcome in these cases .

504
01:10:31,180 --> 01:10:34,580
It is not recommended for other indications.

505
01:10:34,580 --> 01:10:39,868
Similarly, its use as a screening tool for growth-restriction is not advised .

506
01:10:39,868 --> 01:10:52,493
Abnormal umbilical artery Doppler findings should prompt a detailed fetal evaluation, if not already done, because abnormal measurements are often associated with major fetal anomalies and aneuploidy .

507
01:10:52,493 --> 01:10:55,393
# Ductus Arteriosus.

508
01:10:55,393 --> 01:11:04,118
Doppler evaluation of the ductus arteriosus is used primarily to monitor fetuses exposed to indomethacin and other NSAIDs.

509
01:11:04,118 --> 01:11:10,868
Indomethacin may cause ductal constriction or closure, particularly when used in the third trimester .

510
01:11:10,868 --> 01:11:20,618
The resulting increased pulmonary flow can cause reactive hypertrophy of the pulmonary arterioles and eventual development of fetal pulmonary hypertension.

511
01:11:20,618 --> 01:11:33,530
In a review of 12 randomized trials involving more than 200 exposed pregnancies, Koren and coworkers (2006) reported that NSAIDs raised the odds of ductal constriction 15-fold.

512
01:11:33,530 --> 01:11:38,968
The risk is typically limited to drug use greater than 72 hours’ duration.

513
01:11:38,968 --> 01:11:47,918
Monitoring for ductal constriction should be considered in such cases, so that the NSAID can be discontinued if ductal constriction is identified.

514
01:11:47,918 --> 01:11:53,343
Fortunately, effects are often reversible after NSAID discontinuation.

515
01:11:53,343 --> 01:11:55,793
# Uterine Artery.

516
01:11:55,793 --> 01:12:05,518
Uterine blood flow is estimated to rise from 50 mL/min early in gestation to 500 to 750 mL/min by term.

517
01:12:05,518 --> 01:12:12,718
The uterine artery Doppler waveform is characterized by high diastolic flow velocities and turbulent flow.

518
01:12:12,718 --> 01:12:23,618
Greater resistance to flow and development of a diastolic notch are associated with later development of gestational hypertension, preeclampsia, and fetalgrowth restriction.

519
01:12:23,618 --> 01:12:37,643
Zeeman and associates (2003) also found that women with chronic hypertension who had elevated uterine artery impedance at 16 to 20 weeks’ gestation were at greater risk to develop superimposed preeclampsia.

520
01:12:37,643 --> 01:12:45,268
However, the technique, best testing interval, and defining criteria for this indication have not been standardized.

521
01:12:45,268 --> 01:13:05,418
As the predictive value of uterine artery Doppler testing is considered to be low, its use for screening for hypertensive complications of pregnancy or for fetal-growth restriction is not recommended in either high-risk or low-risk pregnancies (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019a, 2020b).

522
01:13:05,418 --> 01:13:08,243
# Middle Cerebral Artery.

523
01:13:08,243 --> 01:13:17,355
Doppler velocimetry of the middle cerebral artery (MCA) has become the primary method of detecting fetal anemia and is central to

524
01:13:17,524 --> 01:13:20,836
surveillance of alloimmunized pregnancies.

525
01:13:20,786 --> 01:13:30,511
Anatomically, the path of the MCA is such that flow often approaches the transducer “head-on,” which allows accurate determination of flow velocity .

526
01:13:30,511 --> 01:13:39,636
The MCA is imaged in an axial view of the head at the base of the skull and ideally within 2 mm of the internal carotid artery origin.

527
01:13:39,636 --> 01:13:48,661
Velocity measurement is optimal when the insonating angle θ is close to zero, and no more than 30 degrees of angle correction should be used.

528
01:13:48,661 --> 01:13:58,399
In general, other fetal vessels are not suitable for velocity assessment, because a larger insonating angle is needed and confers significant measurement error.

529
01:13:58,399 --> 01:14:00,986
Figure 14-11.

530
01:14:00,986 --> 01:14:04,824
Middle cerebral artery (MCA) Doppler.

531
01:14:04,824 --> 01:14:11,036
A. Color Doppler of the circle of Willis demonstrates the correct location to sample the MCA.

532
01:14:11,036 --> 01:14:22,461
B. The waveform shows a peak systolic velocity exceeding 70 cm/sec in a 32-week fetus with severe fetal anemia secondary to Rh alloimmunization.

533
01:14:22,461 --> 01:14:30,336
With fetal anemia, the peak systolic velocity is enhanced due to greater cardiac output and decreased blood viscosity .

534
01:14:30,336 --> 01:14:37,624
This permits the reliable, noninvasive detection of fetal anemia in cases of blood-group alloimmunization.

535
01:14:37,624 --> 01:14:51,261
Mari and colleagues (2000) demonstrated that an MCA peak systolic velocity threshold of 1.50 multiple of the median (MoM) could reliably identify fetuses with moderate or severe anemia.

536
01:14:51,261 --> 01:15:03,599
As discussed in Chapter 18 (p. 355), MCA peak systolic velocity has replaced invasive testing with amniocentesis as the preferred test for fetal anemia detection .

537
01:15:03,599 --> 01:15:09,899
MCA Doppler has also been studied as an adjunct in evaluation of fetalgrowth restriction .

538
01:15:09,899 --> 01:15:20,224
Fetal hypoxemia is associated with increased blood flow to the brain, heart, and adrenal glands, which leads to greater end-diastolic flow in the MCA.

539
01:15:20,224 --> 01:15:35,011
This phenomenon, called “brainsparing,” is actually a misnomer, as it does not protect the fetus but rather is associated with perinatal morbidity and mortality (Bahado-Singh, 1999; Cruz-Martinez, 2011).

540
01:15:35,011 --> 01:15:47,999
To evaluate redistribution of blood flow, investigators have studied a ratio of the MCA pulsatility index (PI) to the umbilical artery PI–termed the cerebroplacental ratio.

541
01:15:47,999 --> 01:15:53,624
Like the S/D ratio, the PI estimates downstream impedance to flow .

542
01:15:53,624 --> 01:16:11,961
In the PORTO (Prospective Observational Trial to Optimize Paediatric Health in IUGR) study of ultrasound-dated pregnancies with estimated fetal weights <10th percentile, fetuses with a cerebroplacental PI ratio <1 had an 11-fold greater risk for adverse perinatal outcomes .

543
01:16:11,961 --> 01:16:16,974
Such findings can further our understanding of fetal pathophysiology.

544
01:16:16,974 --> 01:16:33,749
However, the utility of MCA Doppler to aid the timing of delivery is uncertain, and it has not been adopted as standard practice in the management of growth restriction (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021a; Martins, 2020).

545
01:16:33,749 --> 01:16:36,374
# Ductus Venosus.

546
01:16:36,374 --> 01:16:49,849
The fetal ductus venosus shunts oxygenated blood from the intrahepatic portion of the umbilical vein directly to the inferior vena cava, thus bypassing the liver (Fig. 7-9, p. 127).

547
01:16:49,849 --> 01:16:56,624
The ductus venosus is imaged as it branches from the umbilical vein at approximately the level of the diaphragm.

548
01:16:56,624 --> 01:17:02,574
The waveform is biphasic and normally has forward flow throughout the entire cardiac cycle .

549
01:17:02,574 --> 01:17:09,061
The first peak reflects ventricular systole, and the second is ventricular diastolic filling.

550
01:17:09,061 --> 01:17:14,349
These are followed by a nadir during atrial contraction—termed the a-wave.

551
01:17:14,349 --> 01:17:16,961
Figure 14-12.

552
01:17:16,961 --> 01:17:19,824
Venous Doppler abnormalities.

553
01:17:19,824 --> 01:17:23,849
A. Reversal of a-wave flow in the ductus venosus.

554
01:17:23,849 --> 01:17:27,211
Arrows depict a-waves below the baseline.

555
01:17:27,211 --> 01:17:33,724
This finding may be identified with cardiac dysfunction in the setting of severe fetal-growth restriction.

556
01:17:33,724 --> 01:17:36,936
B. Pulsatile flow in the umbilical vein.

557
01:17:36,936 --> 01:17:43,499
The undulating umbilical venous waveform below the baseline indicates tricuspid regurgitation.

558
01:17:43,499 --> 01:17:50,236
Above the baseline is the umbilical artery waveform, in which there is no visible end-diastolic flow.

559
01:17:50,236 --> 01:17:59,799
Because the venous waveform is below the baseline in this image, it is not possible to determine whether the umbilical artery end-diastolic flow is reversed.

560
01:17:59,799 --> 01:18:05,261
It is believed that Doppler findings in preterm fetuses with growth restriction show

561
01:18:05,428 --> 01:18:13,565
a progression in which umbilical artery Doppler abnormalities are followed by ones in the MCA and then in the ductus venosus.

562
01:18:13,515 --> 01:18:26,803
With severe fetal-growth restriction, cardiac dysfunction may lead to flow in the a-wave that is decreased, absent, and eventually reversed and to pulsatile flow in the umbilical vein (see Fig. 14-12).

563
01:18:26,803 --> 01:18:44,365
However, ductus venosus Doppler assessment has not improved perinatal outcomes, and neither the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021a) nor the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine recommend its use in the routine management of fetal growth-restriction.

564
01:18:44,365 --> 01:18:46,940
Magnetic Resonance Imaging.

565
01:18:46,940 --> 01:18:57,390
MR imaging is based on the excitation of hydrogen ions in the body by pulsing radiofrequencies with high field-strength magnets (1.5 to 3 Tesla).

566
01:18:57,390 --> 01:19:05,290
High-resolution fetal imaging is now possible, in part due to faster acquisition times of less than one second per slice.

567
01:19:05,290 --> 01:19:13,003
The relaxing hydrogen ions can be manipulated through various pulse sequences to produce different representations on images.

568
01:19:13,003 --> 01:19:23,790
The classic example is simple fluid, such as amnionic fluid or urine in the bladder, which appears bright on T2-weighted and dark on T1-weighted image acquisitions.

569
01:19:23,790 --> 01:19:35,803
Image resolution with MR is often superior to that with sonography because MR is less hindered by bony interfaces, maternal obesity, oligohydramnios, or an engaged fetal head.

570
01:19:35,803 --> 01:19:41,803
Thus, it can complement sonography in evaluating suspected fetal abnormalities.

571
01:19:41,803 --> 01:19:51,690
Examples include complex abnormalities of the fetal thorax and of the central nervous, GI, genitourinary, and musculoskeletal systems.

572
01:19:51,690 --> 01:19:57,828
MR imaging is also used to evaluate maternal pelvic masses and placental invasion.

573
01:19:57,828 --> 01:20:07,040
This modality, however, is not portable, is time-consuming, and is generally limited to referral centers with expertise in fetal imaging.

574
01:20:07,040 --> 01:20:18,765
To guide clinical use, the American College of Radiology and Society for Pediatric Radiology (2020b) have developed a practice guideline for fetal MR imaging.

575
01:20:18,765 --> 01:20:24,440
This document acknowledges the primacy of sonography as the preferred screening modality.

576
01:20:24,440 --> 01:20:34,715
Moreover, it recommends that fetal MR imaging be used for problem solving to contribute to prenatal diagnosis, counseling, treatment, and delivery planning.

577
01:20:34,715 --> 01:20:42,215
At Parkland Hospital, MR imaging is performed in 15 percent of pregnancies with major fetal abnormalities .

578
01:20:42,215 --> 01:20:48,190
Specific indications are listed in Table 14-12 and are discussed subsequently .

579
01:20:48,190 --> 01:20:50,715
Table 14-12.

580
01:20:50,715 --> 01:20:56,590
Fetal Conditions for Which Magnetic Resonance Imaging May Be Indicateda # Safety.

581
01:20:56,590 --> 01:21:04,103
MR imaging is not associated with known adverse fetal effects if performed without administration of contrast media .

582
01:21:04,103 --> 01:21:16,928
It uses no ionizing radiation, and human and tissue studies support its safety (Clements, 2000; Reeves, 2010; Vadeyar, 2000; Wiskirchen, 1999).

583
01:21:16,928 --> 01:21:21,178
The strength of the magnetic field is measured in tesla (T).

584
01:21:21,178 --> 01:21:31,715
Both 1.5T and 3T fetal MR imaging can be done safely and successfully, assuming expertise of the imagers with the physical principles of MR .

585
01:21:31,715 --> 01:21:40,953
All clinical examinations must adhere to the specific absorption rate, which is regulated by the FDA, and the ALARA principle should be followed.

586
01:21:40,953 --> 01:21:48,565
Specifically, the amount of heat generated in the MR is reflected in the specific absorption rate and must be monitored.

587
01:21:48,565 --> 01:21:58,490
The Society for Pediatric Radiology recommends 3T MR imaging because of its improved signal-to-noise ratio, which results in better resolution .

588
01:21:58,490 --> 01:22:04,503
We prefer 1.5T for diagnostic imaging given its better field homogeneity.

589
01:22:04,503 --> 01:22:10,003
We reserve 3T for functional imaging, which requires higher field strength.

590
01:22:10,003 --> 01:22:16,240
Gadolinium-based MR contrast agents are gadolinium (Gd3+) chelates.

591
01:22:16,240 --> 01:22:23,665
These contrast agents enter the fetal circulation and are excreted via fetal urination into amnionic fluid.

592
01:22:23,665 --> 01:22:29,615
Here, they may remain for an indeterminate period before being ingested and reabsorbed.

593
01:22:29,615 --> 01:22:41,090
The longer time the gadolinium-chelate molecule remains in a protected space such as the amnionic sac, the greater the potential for dissociation of the toxic Gd3+ ion.

594
01:22:41,090 --> 01:22:50,478
In a population-based review from Ontario, children exposed to gadoliniumenhanced MR imaging in utero were at slightly increased risk for a broad

595
01:22:50,644 --> 01:22:55,969
set of rheumatological, inflammatory, or infiltrative skin conditions .

596
01:22:55,919 --> 01:23:05,931
In adults with renal disease, this contrast agent is associated with development of nephrogenic systemic fibrosis, a potentially severe complication.

597
01:23:05,931 --> 01:23:12,056
MR imaging with gadolinium is not recommended in pregnancy without a life-threatening indication.

598
01:23:12,056 --> 01:23:14,219
# Technique.

599
01:23:14,219 --> 01:23:25,456
Before MR imaging, all women complete a written safety questionnaire that includes information about metallic implants, pacemakers, or other metal- or iron-containing devices.

600
01:23:25,456 --> 01:23:33,169
High-level magnetization may cause dangerous movement in these devices, causing adjacent tissue damage and malfunction.

601
01:23:33,169 --> 01:23:40,606
Iron supplementation may cause image artifact in the colon but does not usually affect the resolution of fetal images.

602
01:23:40,606 --> 01:23:53,544
In more than 4500 MR procedures performed at Parkland Hospital in pregnancy during the past 18 years, <1 percent of our patients suffered maternal anxiety secondary to claustrophobia.

603
01:23:53,544 --> 01:24:03,981
To reduce anxiety in this small group, a single oral dose of diazepam, 5 to 10 mg, or lorazepam, 1 to 2 mg, may be given.

604
01:24:03,981 --> 01:24:10,769
To begin an MR examination, women are placed in a supine or left lateral decubitus position.

605
01:24:10,769 --> 01:24:21,206
A torso coil is used in most circumstances to send and receive the radiofrequency pulses, but a body coil can be used alone to accommodate large maternal habitus.

606
01:24:21,206 --> 01:24:29,981
A series of three-plane localizers, or scout views, are obtained relative to the maternal coronal, sagittal, and axial planes.

607
01:24:29,981 --> 01:24:39,256
The gravid uterus is imaged in the maternal axial plane (7-mm slices, 0 gap) with a T2-weighted fast acquisition.

608
01:24:39,256 --> 01:24:54,506
Typically, these may be a singleshot fast spin echo sequence (SSFSE), half-Fourier acquisition single-shot turbo spin echo (HASTE), or rapid acquisition with relaxation enhancement (RARE), depending on the machine.

609
01:24:54,506 --> 01:25:04,281
Next, a fast T1-weighted acquisition such as spoiled gradient echo (SPGR) is performed (7-mm thickness, 0 gap).

610
01:25:04,281 --> 01:25:12,969
These large-field-of-view acquisitions through the maternal abdomen and pelvis are particularly good for identifying fetal and maternal anatomy.

611
01:25:12,969 --> 01:25:18,169
Orthogonal images of targeted fetal or maternal structures are then obtained.

612
01:25:18,169 --> 01:25:28,806
In these cases, 3- to 5-mm slice thickness, 0 gap T2-weighted acquisitions are performed in the coronal, sagittal, and axial planes.

613
01:25:28,806 --> 01:25:48,881
Depending on the anatomy and underlying suspected abnormality, T1weighted images can be performed to evaluate for subacute hemorrhage, fat, or location of normal structures that appear bright on these sequences, such as liver and meconium in the colon (Brugger, 2006; Zaretsky, 2003b).

614
01:25:48,881 --> 01:26:02,594
Short T1 inversion recovery (STIR) and frequency-selective fat-saturated T2-weighted images may help differentiation in cases in which the water content of the abnormality is similar to that of the normal structure.

615
01:26:02,594 --> 01:26:06,694
An example is a thoracic mass compared with normal lung.

616
01:26:06,694 --> 01:26:20,019
Diffusion-weighted imaging may be employed to evaluate for restricted diffusion, which can be seen in ischemia, cellular tumors, or clotted blood (Brugger, 2006; Zaretsky, 2003b).

617
01:26:20,019 --> 01:26:23,256
# Fetal Anatomy Evaluation.

618
01:26:23,256 --> 01:26:30,806
A fetal anatomical survey is generally completed during the MR examination, regardless of the fetal indication.

619
01:26:30,806 --> 01:26:41,706
Nearly 95 percent of anatomical components recommended by the International Society of Ultrasound in Obstetrics and Gynecology were visible at 30 weeks’ gestation .

620
01:26:41,706 --> 01:26:46,406
The aorta and pulmonary artery were the most difficult to evaluate.

621
01:26:46,406 --> 01:26:58,594
Zaretsky and coworkers (2003a) similarly found that with the exclusion of cardiac structures, fetal anatomical evaluation was possible in 99 percent of cases.

622
01:26:58,594 --> 01:27:01,319
# Central Nervous System.

623
01:27:01,319 --> 01:27:07,769
For intracranial anomalies, fast T2-weighted images produce excellent tissue contrast.

624
01:27:07,769 --> 01:27:18,219
Cerebrospinal fluid–containing structures appear bright, which allows exquisite detail of the posterior fossa, midline structures, and cerebral cortex.

625
01:27:18,219 --> 01:27:22,281
T1-weighted images are used to identify hemorrhage.

626
01:27:22,281 --> 01:27:40,544
CNS biometry obtained with MR imaging is comparable with that obtained using sonography, and nomograms are available for the corpus callosum and cerebellar vermis (Harreld, 2011; Katorza, 2016; Twickler, 2002; Xi, 2016).

627
01:27:40,544 --> 01:27:44,006
MR imaging may provide valuable added

628
01:27:44,164 --> 01:27:53,976
information when cerebral abnormalities are identified or suspected sonographically (Benacerraf, 2007; Li, 2012).

629
01:27:53,926 --> 01:28:01,251
Additional information is more likely to be gained when the examination is performed beyond 24 weeks’ gestation.

630
01:28:01,251 --> 01:28:17,376
In studies, MR imaging has identified additional CNS findings or led to a changed diagnosis in nearly 50 percent of cases and affected prognosis or management in 20 percent (Griffiths, 2017, Twickler, 2003).

631
01:28:17,376 --> 01:28:22,951
MR imaging accurately portrays cerebral gyration and sulcation patterns .

632
01:28:22,951 --> 01:28:37,001
It may assist with identifying agenesis or dysgenesis of the corpus callosum and characterizing migrational abnormalities (Benacerraf, 2007; Li, 2012; Twickler, 2003).

633
01:28:37,001 --> 01:28:45,951
In cases of septo-optic dysplasia, MR imaging may confirm absence of the septum pellucidum and display hypoplastic optic tracts .

634
01:28:45,951 --> 01:28:55,614
When fetal intracranial hemorrhage (ICH) is suspected, MR imaging can help to characterize the extent of bleeding and to estimate when bleeding occurred.

635
01:28:55,614 --> 01:29:08,664
Risk factors for fetal ICH include atypical-appearing ventriculomegaly, concern for neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia, and severe TTTS or demise of one monochorionic twin .

636
01:29:08,664 --> 01:29:18,014
With congenital fetal infections, MR imaging can delineate the variable degrees of neural parenchymal abnormality and subsequent maldevelopment .

637
01:29:18,014 --> 01:29:20,801
Figure 14-13.

638
01:29:20,801 --> 01:29:28,076
Axial images of the fetal brain demonstrate the normal gyration and sulcation progression during fetal development.

639
01:29:28,076 --> 01:29:30,901
A. 23 weeks’ gestation.

640
01:29:30,901 --> 01:29:33,739
B. 33 weeks’ gestation.

641
01:29:33,739 --> 01:29:43,526
These images were obtained using a Half Fourier Acquisition Single Shot Turbo Spin Echo (HASTE) sequence because it is relatively motion insensitive.

642
01:29:43,526 --> 01:29:46,289
Figure 14-14.

643
01:29:46,289 --> 01:29:48,739
Septo-optic dysplasia.

644
01:29:48,739 --> 01:29:50,689
A. Axial.

645
01:29:50,689 --> 01:29:52,664
B. Coronal.

646
01:29:52,664 --> 01:29:59,664
Images at 30 weeks’ gestation confirm absence of the cavum septum pellucidum (arrowheads) in both.

647
01:29:59,664 --> 01:30:04,051
There is also associated mild ventriculomegaly (arrow).

648
01:30:04,051 --> 01:30:06,264
# Thorax.

649
01:30:06,264 --> 01:30:14,401
MR imaging can delineate the location and size of space-occupying thoracic lesions and quantify the volume of lung tissue.

650
01:30:14,401 --> 01:30:23,676
If needed, it can characterize blood flow to a thoracic mass to differentiate cystic adenomatoid malformation from pulmonary sequestration .

651
01:30:23,676 --> 01:30:37,626
With congenital diaphragmatic hernia, MR imaging can help verify and quantify the abdominal organs within the thorax (Debus, 2013; Lee, 2011; MehollinRay, 2012).

652
01:30:37,626 --> 01:30:42,414
This includes the volume of herniated liver and of compressed lung tissue .

653
01:30:42,414 --> 01:30:56,451
MR imaging can also measure lung volumes in pregnancies with prolonged oligohydramnios secondary to renal abnormalities or ruptured membranes (Messerschmidt, 2011; Zaretsky, 2005).

654
01:30:56,451 --> 01:30:59,176
Figure 14-15.

655
01:30:59,176 --> 01:31:05,039
A. Coronal image of normal lungs on a balanced sequence at 29 weeks’ gestation.

656
01:31:05,039 --> 01:31:09,701
The liver (L) and stomach (S) lie below the diaphragm.

657
01:31:09,701 --> 01:31:18,139
B. Left-sided congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) (dotted ellipse) seen on balanced sequence at 33 weeks.

658
01:31:18,139 --> 01:31:31,351
C. The T1-weighted sequence confirms the subdiaphragmatic position of the liver and better delineates the small bowel (arrow) and meconium-containing colon (arrowhead) that have herniated into the chest.

659
01:31:31,351 --> 01:31:43,026
D. Another image of a leftsided CDH at 22 weeks demonstrates no normal lung, the heart (H) displaced into the right chest, and an elevated liver (dotted ellipse).

660
01:31:43,026 --> 01:31:45,114
# Abdomen.

661
01:31:45,114 --> 01:31:53,601
Meconium accumulation within the GI tract gives a predictable pattern and has high signal intensity on T1-weighted sequences.

662
01:31:53,601 --> 01:32:01,639
Thus, MR imaging is a complementary tool in diagnosing GI abnormalities and complex cloacal malformations .

663
01:32:01,639 --> 01:32:11,639
With cystic abdominal abnormalities, differences in signal characteristics may also help distinguish between meconium in the fetal colon and urine in the bladder .

664
01:32:11,639 --> 01:32:25,014
Peritoneal calcifications related to meconium peritonitis are more readily apparent sonographically, whereas pseudocysts and resultant abnormalities of meconium migration are better delineated with MR imaging.

665
01:32:25,014 --> 01:32:28,126
# Adjunct to Fetal Therapy.

666
01:32:28,126 --> 01:32:32,414
Prior to fetal surgery, MR imaging is often used.

667
01:32:32,414 --> 01:32:38,226
In cases of myelomeningocele, it precisely displays brain and spine anatomy.

668
01:32:38,226 --> 01:32:52,764
For sacrococcygeal teratomas, if fetal surgery is considered, MR imaging may identify tumor extension into the fetal pelvis (Avni, 2002; Neubert, 2004; Perrone, 2018).

669
01:32:52,924 --> 01:33:02,624
When a fetal neck mass is identified with ultrasound, MR imaging may help delineate a lesion’s extent and its mass effect on the oral cavity and trachea.

670
01:33:02,574 --> 01:33:17,086
This can help identify cases that are at risk for adverse outcome and that may benefit from an ex utero intrapartum treatment (EXIT) procedure (Lazar, 2012; Ogamo, 2005; Ng, 2019).

671
01:33:17,086 --> 01:33:28,011
MR imaging can also calculate a jaw index when an EXIT procedure may be needed for severe micrognathia (Kooiman, 2018; Morris, 2009).

672
01:33:28,011 --> 01:33:43,286
At some centers, before laser ablation of placental anastomoses for TTTS, MR imaging is performed to identify fetal ICH or periventricular leukomalacia (Hu, 2006; Kline-Fath, 2007).

673
01:33:43,286 --> 01:33:48,549
Fetal therapy is discussed in Chapter 19 (p. 367).

674
01:33:48,549 --> 01:33:50,674
# Placenta.

675
01:33:50,674 --> 01:34:00,961
Despite improvements in ultrasound detection of PAS, particularly when the placenta is anterior, characterization of the extent of invasion remains a challenge.

676
01:34:00,961 --> 01:34:06,774
MR findings concerning for invasion are depicted in Figure 14-16.

677
01:34:06,774 --> 01:34:22,349
They include dark intraplacental bands on T2-weighted images, a focal bulge, placental heterogeneity, involvement of the bladder-uterine serosal interface, and fibrin deposition (Clark, 2020; Leyendecker, 2012).

678
01:34:22,349 --> 01:34:28,611
When used to complement ultrasound, MR imaging’s sensitivity to detect invasion is high.

679
01:34:28,611 --> 01:34:40,161
Clark and associates (2020) reported that MR imaging findings positively correlated with need for cesarean hysterectomy and with histological and surgical impressions of invasion.

680
01:34:40,161 --> 01:34:48,936
Clinical risk factors and ultrasound findings (p. 253) should be incorporated when interpreting MR placental images .

681
01:34:48,936 --> 01:34:51,636
Figure 14-16.

682
01:34:51,636 --> 01:35:00,336
A. Sagittal T2-weighted image of the placenta demonstrates placenta accreta spectrum (PAS), in this case placenta percreta.

683
01:35:00,336 --> 01:35:12,199
There are intraplacental, dark, linear bands (arrows), a large bulge along the bladder-serosal interface (arrowheads), and a central area of marked inhomogeneity (asterisk).

684
01:35:12,199 --> 01:35:22,299
B. A coronal image from the same study demonstrates the bulge with right lateral extension (arrowheads) and multiple, dark, linear bands (arrows).

685
01:35:22,299 --> 01:35:34,774
C. A sagittal, balanced, steady state free precession (SSFE) image shows a bulge, retroplacental vessels, and irregularity of the bladder-serosal interface (arrowheads).

686
01:35:34,774 --> 01:35:39,061
These contrast with the large maternal varices (arrows).

687
01:35:39,061 --> 01:35:41,224
F = fetus.

688
01:35:41,224 --> 01:35:48,111
Two emerging technologies also are being employed to investigate placental insufficiency and PAS.

689
01:35:48,111 --> 01:35:57,611
Arterial spin labelling is a technique for functional assessment of perfusion and excites maternal red blood cells to serve as endogenous contrast agents .

690
01:35:57,611 --> 01:36:03,936
Second, radiomics textural analysis characterizes placental morphology in vivo.

691
01:36:03,936 --> 01:36:09,774
MR textural features may help select pregnancies requiring cesarean hysterectomy .

